window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS = window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS || {};

window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS["csharp-01"] = "# Lesson 01: .NET Architecture, Value vs Reference Types, Boxing & Strings\n\n> **Target Audience:** 5-Year Full Stack .NET Developer (ECE Background, 5+ Real Projects)\n> **Goal:** Master the core memory management, runtime architecture, and string handling questions asked in the first 15 minutes of every technical interview.\n\n---\n\n## Q1: What is the .NET CLR, and how does it execute C# code (IL, JIT Compiler)?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nThe **CLR (Common Language Runtime)** is the execution engine of .NET—like a virtual machine that takes your compiled C# code, converts it into machine code using the **JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler**, and manages memory, security, and garbage collection.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When we write C# code and build the project, it does not compile directly into OS-specific machine code. Instead, it follows a two-step compilation process:\n1. **Compile Time (C# Compiler / Roslyn):** The C# compiler converts our `.cs` files into **IL (Intermediate Language)** or MSIL, packaged inside an assembly (`.dll` or `.exe`). This IL code is CPU-independent.\n2. **Run Time (CLR & JIT):** When the application runs, the **CLR** takes over. Inside the CLR, the **JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler** compiles the IL code into native machine code (CPU instructions) right when a method is called for the first time, taking into account the exact OS and CPU architecture (x64, ARM64).\n\nBeyond JIT compilation, the CLR is responsible for **Automatic Memory Management (Garbage Collection)**, **Exception Handling**, **Type Safety verification**, and **Thread Management**.\"\n\n### Execution Flow Diagram\n```\n[ C# Source Code (.cs) ]\n         │\n         ▼  (Roslyn C# Compiler - csc.exe)\n[ Intermediate Language (IL / .dll / .exe) ]\n         │\n         ▼  (Loaded into CLR at Runtime)\n[ JIT Compiler (Just-In-Time) ]\n         │\n         ▼  (Converts to Native CPU Instructions)\n[ Native Machine Code (010101) ] ──► Executed by OS / CPU\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q2: What is the difference between CTS (Common Type System) and CLS (Common Language Specification)?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**CTS** defines the master list of all data types supported by the .NET runtime so different .NET languages share the same types, while **CLS** is a strict subset of rules that languages must follow if they want their code to be callable by other .NET languages.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Both CTS and CLS exist to support **language interoperability** in .NET (for example, allowing a C# project to use a library written in VB.NET or F#):\n- **CTS (Common Type System):** It is the standard that defines how data types are declared, used, and managed in the CLR. For example, in C# we write `int`, and in VB.NET someone writes `Integer`. Because of CTS, both compile down to the exact same underlying structure: `System.Int32`. CTS divides all types into two categories: **Value Types** and **Reference Types**.\n- **CLS (Common Language Specification):** It is a subset of CTS rules. Different languages have unique features—for example, C# is case-sensitive and supports unsigned integers (`uint`), whereas VB.NET historically was not case-sensitive. If you build a public NuGet library and follow CLS rules (e.g., not exposing two public methods that differ only by case), any .NET language is guaranteed to be able to consume your library.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class CtsDemo\n{\n    public void ShowTypeUnification()\n    {\n        // 'int' is a C# keyword alias, 'System.Int32' is the CTS type\n        int csharpInt = 100;\n        System.Int32 ctsInt = 100;\n\n        Console.WriteLine(csharpInt.GetType() == ctsInt.GetType()); // Output: True\n        Console.WriteLine(csharpInt.GetType().FullName);            // Output: System.Int32\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q3: What is Managed Code vs Unmanaged Code in .NET?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**Managed code** runs inside the CLR sandbox where memory and garbage collection are handled automatically, whereas **unmanaged code** runs outside the CLR directly on the OS (like C++ DLLs, Win32 APIs, file handles, or raw database connections) where memory must be freed manually.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **Managed Code:** Any code written in C# or VB.NET that targets the CLR. The CLR manages its full lifecycle: allocating memory on the Managed Heap, checking array bounds, verifying type safety, and cleaning up unused objects via the **Garbage Collector (GC)**.\n- **Unmanaged Code:** Code compiled directly to native machine code that executes outside the CLR's control—such as old C/C++ libraries, COM components, OS file handles, network sockets, or raw database connections.\n- **Real Project Relevance:** Even in a pure C# Web API, when our code opens a `SqlConnection`, reads a file via `FileStream`, or processes an image using `System.Drawing`, the C# wrapper object is managed, but it holds an underlying **unmanaged OS handle**. Because the Garbage Collector does not know how to free unmanaged resources automatically, we must clean them up using `IDisposable` and the `using` statement.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.IO;\n\npublic class ResourceDemo\n{\n    public void ReadFileSafely()\n    {\n        // FileStream wraps an UNMANAGED OS file handle.\n        // Using the 'using' statement ensures the unmanaged file handle is closed immediately.\n        using var stream = new FileStream(\"report.pdf\", FileMode.Open);\n        // Work with stream...\n    } // stream.Dispose() is automatically called here!\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q4: Explain Value Types vs Reference Types with Stack and Heap memory allocation.\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**Value Types** store their actual data directly in their own memory slot (usually on the **Stack**) and copy by value, while **Reference Types** store the actual object on the **Heap** and hold only a memory address (pointer) to that object on the Stack.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In C#, every data type is either a Value Type or a Reference Type:\n1. **Value Types (`struct`, `enum`, `int`, `double`, `bool`, `decimal`, `DateTime`):**\n   - They hold the actual value directly.\n   - When declared as local variables inside a method, they are stored on the **Stack** (fast LIFO memory cleaned up immediately when the method exits).\n   - When you assign one value type variable to another (`b = a`), a **complete independent copy** of the value is created. Changing `b` never affects `a`.\n2. **Reference Types (`class`, `interface`, `delegate`, `record`, `object`, `string`, Arrays):**\n   - The actual object data is allocated on the **Managed Heap** (cleaned up later by the Garbage Collector), and the variable on the **Stack** holds only the memory address (reference) pointing to that Heap location.\n   - When you assign one reference variable to another (`emp2 = emp1`), both variables point to the **exact same object on the Heap**. Modifying `emp2.Name` will also change `emp1.Name`.\n\n*(Bonus 5-Year Senior Point)*: If a Value Type (like `int Age`) is declared as a property inside a Class (`Employee`), that `int` lives on the **Heap** as part of the `Employee` object!\"\n\n### Memory Diagram\n```\nSTACK MEMORY (Fast, Thread-Local)          MANAGED HEAP (Shared, GC Managed)\n┌─────────────────────────────┐            ┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│ int a = 10;                 │            │                                 │\n│ int b = 20; (Copy of a+10)  │            │  [ Employee Object @ 0x90A1 ]   │\n├─────────────────────────────┤            │  ├── Id: 101                    │\n│ emp1 = 0x90A1 ──────────────┼───────────►│  └── Name: \"Ravi\"               │\n│ emp2 = 0x90A1 ──────────────┼───────────►│                                 │\n└─────────────────────────────┘            └─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class Employee\n{\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class MemoryDemo\n{\n    public void RunDemo()\n    {\n        // 1. VALUE TYPE: Copies actual value\n        int a = 10;\n        int b = a;\n        b = 99;\n        Console.WriteLine($\"a = {a}, b = {b}\"); // a = 10, b = 99 (Independent!)\n\n        // 2. REFERENCE TYPE: Copies memory address\n        var emp1 = new Employee { Name = \"Karthik\" };\n        var emp2 = emp1; // Both point to the same Heap object\n        emp2.Name = \"Suresh\";\n\n        Console.WriteLine($\"emp1.Name = {emp1.Name}\"); // Output: Suresh (Modified!)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q5: Can a Value Type be stored on the Heap? Can a Reference Type be stored on the Stack?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nYes! A Value Type is stored on the **Heap** when it is a field inside a class, inside an array, or when it is **boxed**. Conversely, modern .NET JIT can sometimes allocate small objects on the stack via **Span\\<T\\>** or `stackalloc`.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"A common misconception is that 'Value types always live on the Stack.' In reality, storage location depends on the **context** in which the value type is declared:\n1. **When Value Types go to the Heap:**\n   - **Class Fields:** If an `Order` class has `public decimal TotalAmount { get; set; }`, when `new Order()` is created on the Heap, `TotalAmount` lives right inside that Heap block.\n   - **Boxing:** Assigning an `int` to an `object` allocates a wrapper on the Heap.\n   - **Closures / Async Methods:** Local variables captured by a lambda expression or used across `await` boundaries are hoisted into a compiler-generated state-machine class on the Heap.\n2. **Stack Allocation for High Performance:**\n   - Using `stackalloc` with `Span<T>`, we can allocate contiguous buffers on the Stack without creating Heap garbage.\"\n\n---\n\n## Q6: What is Boxing and Unboxing in C#? What is its performance impact and how do we avoid it?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**Boxing** is converting a Value Type (`int`) into a Reference Type (`object`) by wrapping it on the Heap; **Unboxing** is extracting that Value Type back from the `object` to the Stack. It hurts performance, so we use **Generics (`List<T>`)** to avoid it.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **Boxing:** It is the implicit conversion of a Value Type (`int`, `struct`) to `System.Object` or to an interface implemented by the value type. When boxing happens, the CLR allocates a new object on the **Managed Heap** and copies the value from the Stack into that Heap object.\n- **Unboxing:** It is the explicit conversion of the boxed `object` reference back into a Value Type. The CLR first verifies that the runtime type inside the box matches the requested value type (throwing `InvalidCastException` if it doesn't), and then copies the value from the Heap back to the Stack.\n- **Why it is bad for performance:** Boxing is up to **20x slower** than a normal assignment because it requires Heap memory allocation and creates extra objects that the Garbage Collector later has to clean up (increasing GC pressure).\n- **How we avoid it in Real Projects:**\n  1. Never use legacy non-generic collections like `ArrayList` or `Hashtable`. Always use generic collections like `List<T>` and `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`.\n  2. Use generic methods (`void Log<T>(T item)`) instead of taking `object` parameters.\n  3. Use string interpolation (`$\"Count: {count}\"`) in modern .NET or `.ToString()` instead of passing value types to `string.Format(string, object)` overloads.\"\n\n### Boxing & Unboxing Memory Diagram\n```\nBOXING (object obj = num;)                   UNBOXING (int x = (int)obj;)\nStack                 Heap                   Stack                 Heap\n┌─────────┐           ┌──────────────┐       ┌─────────┐           ┌──────────────┐\n│ num: 42 ├──────────►│ Boxed Int32  │       │ x: 42   │◄──────────┤ Boxed Int32  │\n│ obj: ───┼──────────►│ Value = 42   │       │ obj: ───┼──────────►│ Value = 42   │\n└─────────┘  Copies   └──────────────┘       └─────────┘  Copies   └──────────────┘\n             to Heap                                      to Stack\n```\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class BoxingDemo\n{\n    public void ComparePerformance()\n    {\n        int number = 500;\n\n        // 1. BOXING (Implicit - Allocates on Heap!)\n        object boxedObj = number;\n\n        // 2. UNBOXING (Explicit cast required - Copies back to Stack!)\n        int unboxedNumber = (int)boxedObj;\n\n        // ❌ BAD: ArrayList stores 'object', so adding an int causes BOXING every time!\n        ArrayList badList = new ArrayList();\n        badList.Add(10); // Boxed!\n        int val1 = (int)badList[0]; // Unboxed!\n\n        // ✅ GOOD: List<int> is strongly typed via Generics. Zero boxing!\n        List<int> goodList = new List<int>();\n        goodList.Add(10); // Stored as raw int!\n        int val2 = goodList[0]; // No cast, no unboxing!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q7: Why is `string` a Reference Type in C#, yet it behaves like a Value Type? Why are strings immutable?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`string` is a Reference Type stored on the Heap because text can be arbitrarily large, but it is **immutable** (cannot be changed in place after creation). Any modification creates a brand-new string object on the Heap.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"`string` (`System.String`) is a Reference Type—internally it is stored as a contiguous array of `char` on the Heap. However, whenever you modify a string (`s1 += \" world\"`), the original string in memory is **not** changed. Instead, the CLR allocates a **brand-new string object** on the Heap with the combined text and updates your variable to point to the new address.\n\n**Three reasons why Microsoft designed `string` to be immutable:**\n1. **String Interning (Memory Saving):** The CLR maintains an internal hash table called the **String Intern Pool**. If the literal `\"Active\"` appears 5,000 times in your application, the CLR stores `\"Active\"` only **once** in memory and points all 5,000 references to the same address. If strings were mutable, changing one variable would corrupt the other 4,999 variables!\n2. **Thread Safety:** Because strings cannot be modified after creation, multiple threads can read and share the same string instance simultaneously without locks or race conditions.\n3. **Security & HashCode Caching:** Strings are used for database connection strings, file paths, URLs, and `Dictionary` keys. Immutability guarantees that a dictionary key's hash code never changes after insertion, and no method can maliciously alter a validated file path or connection string.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class StringImmutabilityDemo\n{\n    public void TestStrings()\n    {\n        string s1 = \"DotNet\";\n        string s2 = s1; // Both s1 and s2 point to \"DotNet\" on the Heap\n\n        // Modifying s1 does NOT change s2! It creates a NEW string on the Heap.\n        s1 = s1 + \" Core\";\n\n        Console.WriteLine(s1); // Output: DotNet Core\n        Console.WriteLine(s2); // Output: DotNet (Unchanged!)\n\n        // String Interning check: Two identical string literals share the exact same memory address!\n        string a = \"Active\";\n        string b = \"Active\";\n        Console.WriteLine(object.ReferenceEquals(a, b)); // Output: True\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q8: What is the difference between `string` and `StringBuilder`? When should you use which?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`string` is immutable and creates a new Heap object on every modification, whereas `StringBuilder` is mutable and modifies an internal character buffer in-place without creating garbage objects.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **How `string` works:** If you concatenate a string inside a `for` loop running 10,000 times (`result += item;`), it creates **10,000 separate intermediate string objects** on the Heap, copying all previous characters every single time ($O(N^2)$ time and huge GC pressure).\n- **How `StringBuilder` works:** `System.Text.StringBuilder` maintains a mutable internal `char[]` buffer (default capacity is 16 characters, which doubles automatically when full). When you call `.Append()`, it simply writes characters into the existing buffer without allocating new string objects until you finally call `.ToString()`.\n- **When to use which in Real Projects:**\n  - Use **`string`** when concatenating a small, fixed number of strings (e.g., 2 to 4 strings like `firstName + \" \" + lastName` or string interpolation `$\"User {id}\"`), because the compiler optimizes a single statement into a single `string.Concat` call.\n  - Use **`StringBuilder`** whenever you are building strings dynamically inside a **loop** (`for`/`foreach`), generating **dynamic CSV/HTML email bodies**, building **dynamic SQL/log payloads**, or concatenating dozens of pieces of text.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `string` (`System.String`) | `StringBuilder` (`System.Text.StringBuilder`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mutability** | **Immutable** (Read-only in memory) | **Mutable** (Modifies internal buffer in-place) |\n| **Memory Allocation** | Allocates a new Heap object on every change | Reuses internal buffer; expands capacity only when full |\n| **Thread Safety** | Thread-safe by default | Not thread-safe |\n| **Best Use Case** | Fixed/small concatenations (1–4 operations) | Loops, dynamic CSV/HTML generation, large text assembly |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Text;\n\npublic class CsvExportService\n{\n    // Real Project Example: Exporting 5,000 orders to a CSV file\n    public string GenerateOrdersCsv(List<(int Id, string Customer, decimal Total)> orders)\n    {\n        // Pre-sizing capacity avoids even buffer resizing overhead\n        var sb = new StringBuilder(capacity: orders.Count * 32);\n        sb.AppendLine(\"OrderId,CustomerName,TotalAmount\");\n\n        foreach (var order in orders)\n        {\n            // Zero intermediate string allocations in the loop!\n            sb.Append(order.Id)\n              .Append(',')\n              .Append(order.Customer)\n              .Append(',')\n              .Append(order.Total)\n              .AppendLine();\n        }\n\n        return sb.ToString(); // Allocates the final string only ONCE\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q9: What is the difference between `==` and `.Equals()` in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nFor **Reference Types**, `==` compares memory addresses (reference equality) while `.Equals()` can be overridden to compare content; however, **`string` is a special exception** where both `==` and `.Equals()` compare the actual text value!\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"The behavior of `==` and `.Equals()` depends on whether we are comparing Value Types, Reference Types, or `string`:\n1. **For Value Types (`int`, `bool`, `struct`):** Both `==` and `.Equals()` compare the **actual values** (e.g., `5 == 5` is `true`).\n2. **For Custom Reference Types (`class`):** By default, both `==` and `object.Equals()` check **Reference Equality**—meaning they return `true` only if both variables point to the exact same memory address on the Heap. However, `==` is an operator (resolved at compile time based on the variable's declared type), whereas `.Equals()` is a virtual method (resolved at runtime via polymorphism).\n3. **For `string` (And C# 9+ `record` types):** Microsoft overloaded the `==` operator and overrode `.Equals()` on `System.String` so that **both compare the actual character content**, not the memory address.\n4. **Tricky Interview Trap (`object` vs `string`):** If you store two strings with identical text (created separately at runtime) inside `object` variables (`object a = ...; object b = ...;`), then `a == b` will use `object`'s `==` operator (comparing memory addresses, returning `false`), whereas `a.Equals(b)` will call `string.Equals()` virtually at runtime (comparing values, returning `true`)!\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class EqualityTrapDemo\n{\n    public void ShowInterviewTrap()\n    {\n        // Create two separate string instances on the Heap with the same characters\n        object obj1 = new string(new char[] { 'A', 'P', 'I' });\n        object obj2 = new string(new char[] { 'A', 'P', 'I' });\n\n        // '==' checks compile-time type (object) -> compares memory addresses!\n        Console.WriteLine(obj1 == obj2);      // Output: False (Different Heap addresses!)\n\n        // '.Equals()' is a virtual method -> calls string.Equals() at runtime -> compares content!\n        Console.WriteLine(obj1.Equals(obj2)); // Output: True (Same text \"API\"!)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q10: What are Nullable Value Types (`int?` / `Nullable<T>`) and why do we need them?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nNormally, Value Types (`int`, `DateTime`, `bool`) cannot hold `null`. Adding a `?` (e.g., `int?` or `DateTime?`) wraps the value type in `System.Nullable<T>`, allowing it to represent either a valid value or `null` (missing database value).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"By default, a Value Type like `int` must always have a value (its default is `0`), and `DateTime` defaults to `01-01-0001`.\nHowever, in real-world applications and SQL databases, a column like `DispatchedDate` or `ParentCategoryId` can genuinely be `NULL` (meaning 'not yet dispatched' or 'no parent'). Using `0` is dangerous because `0` might be a valid ID or score.\n\nC# solves this with `Nullable<T>` (shorthand `T?`, such as `int?`, `bool?`, `DateTime?`). Internally, `Nullable<T>` is a `struct` with two key properties:\n- `HasValue` (`bool`): Returns `true` if a value is present, `false` if it is `null`.\n- `Value` (`T`): Returns the underlying value (or throws `InvalidOperationException` if `HasValue` is `false`).\n- `GetValueOrDefault()`: Safely returns the value if present, or the default value (`0`) if `null`.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class OrderEntity\n{\n    public int OrderId { get; set; }\n    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }\n    public DateTime? DeliveredAt { get; set; } // Nullable: NULL until order is delivered!\n}\n\npublic class NullableDemo\n{\n    public void CheckDelivery(OrderEntity order)\n    {\n        if (order.DeliveredAt.HasValue)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"Delivered on: {order.DeliveredAt.Value:yyyy-MM-dd}\");\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine(\"Order is still in transit.\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q11: Explain Null-Conditional (`?.`, `?[]`), Null-Coalescing (`??`), and Null-Coalescing Assignment (`??=`) operators.\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- `?.` safely accesses properties/methods only if the object is not null (preventing `NullReferenceException`).\n- `??` provides a fallback default value when the left side is `null`.\n- `??=` assigns a value to a variable only if that variable is currently `null`.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In modern C#, we use these three operators daily to write clean, defensive code and avoid `NullReferenceException` without deeply nested `if (x != null)` blocks:\n1. **Null-Conditional Operator (`?.` and `?[]`):** Evaluates the left-hand object; if it is `null`, it short-circuits the entire expression and returns `null` instead of throwing a `NullReferenceException`.\n2. **Null-Coalescing Operator (`??`):** Returns the left-hand operand if it is **not null**; otherwise, it evaluates and returns the right-hand fallback operand.\n3. **Null-Coalescing Assignment Operator (`??=`):** Introduced in C# 8.0, `list ??= new List<string>();` initializes the variable on the left **only if** it is currently `null`.\n\nIn our Web API controllers and services, we frequently combine `?.` and `??` in a single line (e.g., `string city = user?.Address?.City ?? \"Hyderabad\";`).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class CustomerProfile\n{\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n    public Address ShippingAddress { get; set; }\n    public List<string> Tags { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class Address\n{\n    public string City { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class NullOperatorsDemo\n{\n    public string GetCustomerCity(CustomerProfile customer)\n    {\n        // 1. Combining ?. and ??\n        // If customer is null OR ShippingAddress is null OR City is null -> returns \"Unknown\"\n        string city = customer?.ShippingAddress?.City ?? \"Unknown\";\n\n        // 2. Using ??= to lazily initialize a null list\n        if (customer != null)\n        {\n            customer.Tags ??= new List<string>();\n            customer.Tags.Add(\"Verified\");\n        }\n\n        return city;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q12: What is the difference between `is` and `as` operators in C# (including Pattern Matching)?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nThe `is` operator checks whether an object is compatible with a given type (returning `bool` and optionally casting it into a variable), while the `as` operator attempts to cast a Reference/Nullable type and returns `null` instead of throwing an exception if the cast fails.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When working with polymorphism or `object` references, a direct cast like `(AdminUser)user` will throw an `InvalidCastException` at runtime if the object is not actually an `AdminUser`. To cast safely:\n1. **`is` Operator (with Pattern Matching):** Checks runtime type compatibility and returns `true` or `false`. Since C# 7.0, we can combine the check and cast in one step: `if (obj is AdminUser admin)`. This checks the type and immediately gives us a strongly-typed `admin` variable—and it works for **both Value Types and Reference Types**.\n2. **`as` Operator:** Attempts to cast an object to a **Reference Type or Nullable Type**. If the cast succeeds, it returns the typed reference; if it fails, it returns `null` without throwing an exception (which is much faster than catching an `InvalidCastException`).\n\nIn modern C#, `if (obj is Employee emp)` is generally preferred over `as` followed by a `null` check because it is cleaner and works with value types too.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class User { public string Email { get; set; } }\npublic class AdminUser : User { public string RoleLevel { get; set; } }\n\npublic class CastingDemo\n{\n    public void ProcessUser(User currentUser)\n    {\n        // ✅ Modern C# Pattern Matching with 'is' (Checks type AND casts in one step)\n        if (currentUser is AdminUser admin)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"Admin Access: {admin.RoleLevel}\");\n        }\n\n        // ✅ Using 'as' operator (Returns null if currentUser is not an AdminUser)\n        AdminUser maybeAdmin = currentUser as AdminUser;\n        if (maybeAdmin != null)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"Admin Email: {maybeAdmin.Email}\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n";

window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS["csharp-02"] = "# Lesson 02: C# Keywords — `const`, `readonly`, `ref`, `out`, `var`, `dynamic`, `static`, `sealed` & Access Modifiers\n\n> **Target Audience:** 5-Year Full Stack .NET Developer (ECE Background, 5+ Real Projects)\n> **Goal:** Give crisp, unambiguous comparisons for every C# keyword comparison question asked in technical interviews.\n\n---\n\n## Q1: What is the difference between `const` and `readonly` in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`const` is a **compile-time constant** whose value is hardcoded at declaration and baked directly into the IL code, whereas `readonly` is a **run-time constant** that can be initialized either at declaration or inside a constructor (and can hold different values per object instance or be loaded from config).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"This is one of the most common interview questions, and I always break it down into four key differences:\n1. **Initialization Time:** A `const` field must be initialized at the exact line it is declared (compile-time). A `readonly` field can be initialized at declaration **or** inside the class constructor at run-time.\n2. **Supported Data Types:** `const` only supports primitive built-in types (`int`, `double`, `bool`, `char`), `enum`, and `string` (unless the reference type is assigned `null`). `readonly` supports **any type**, including custom classes, interfaces (`readonly ILogger _logger`), `DateTime.UtcNow`, and `List<T>`.\n3. **`static` Behavior:** `const` is **implicitly `static`**—you access it via `ClassName.ConstName`, never through an object instance. `readonly` is an instance-level field by default (so two instances can have different `readonly` values set via constructor), unless you explicitly mark it `static readonly`.\n4. **DLL Versioning Pitfall (5-Year Senior Point):** When Assembly A uses a `const` from Assembly B, the C# compiler copies the raw literal value directly into Assembly A's IL code. If Assembly B later changes that `const` value and you deploy only `AssemblyB.dll` without recompiling Assembly A, Assembly A will **still use the old value**! `static readonly` reads the memory address at runtime, avoiding this bug.\n\n**Real Project Example:** In our ASP.NET Core Web APIs, we inject dependencies into controllers/services as `private readonly IOrderRepository _orderRepo;` so that no method can accidentally overwrite `_orderRepo = null;` after the constructor finishes.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `const` | `readonly` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Evaluated At** | **Compile-time** | **Run-time** |\n| **Where Initialized** | At declaration only | At declaration **or** inside the Constructor |\n| **Modifier** | Implicitly `static` (cannot use `static const`) | Instance-level by default (can use `static readonly`) |\n| **Allowed Types** | Primitives (`int`, `double`), `string`, `enum` | Any type (`class`, `interface`, `struct`, `DateTime`) |\n| **Memory / IL** | Baked directly as literal into caller's IL | Stored as a field in memory; resolved at runtime |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class OrderService\n{\n    // 1. CONST: Known at compile time, implicitly static\n    public const double MaxTaxRate = 0.18;\n\n    // 2. STATIC READONLY: Evaluated once at runtime when class is first loaded\n    public static readonly DateTime ServiceStartTime = DateTime.UtcNow;\n\n    // 3. INSTANCE READONLY: Assigned at runtime via Constructor (e.g., Dependency Injection)\n    private readonly string _connectionString;\n\n    public OrderService(string connectionString)\n    {\n        // Allowed in constructor!\n        _connectionString = connectionString;\n    }\n\n    public void Process()\n    {\n        // _connectionString = \"new value\"; // ❌ Compiler Error: A readonly field cannot be assigned to!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q2: If a `List<string>` or custom object is marked `readonly`, can we still add items to it or modify its properties?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**Yes!** Marking a Reference Type as `readonly` only locks the **reference (memory pointer)** so you cannot reassign `myList = new List<string>()`, but you can still freely modify the internal contents (`myList.Add(\"item\")`) of the object on the Heap.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When we mark a reference type field as `readonly`—for example, `private readonly List<string> _roles = new();`—the `readonly` keyword only prevents reassigning the variable to point to a **different** object on the Heap after the constructor finishes. It does **not** make the object itself immutable! We can still call `_roles.Add(\"Admin\")` or `_roles.Clear()`. If we want true immutability for a collection, we should expose `IReadOnlyList<T>` or `ImmutableList<T>`.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class RoleManager\n{\n    private readonly List<string> _roles = new List<string> { \"User\" };\n\n    public void UpdateRoles()\n    {\n        // ✅ ALLOWED: Modifying the contents inside the Heap object\n        _roles.Add(\"Admin\");\n\n        // ❌ COMPILER ERROR: Cannot reassign the reference pointer itself!\n        // _roles = new List<string>();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q3: What is the difference between `ref`, `out`, and `in` parameters in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nAll three pass arguments **by reference** (sharing the caller's variable memory instead of copying), where **`ref`** is two-way (input & output), **`out`** is output-only (method must assign it), and **`in`** is read-only input (method cannot modify it).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"By default in C#, parameters are passed **by value** (a copy of the variable is passed). When we use `ref`, `out`, or `in`, we pass the variable **by reference** (passing a pointer to the caller's actual variable):\n1. **`ref` (Two-Way: Read & Write):**\n   - The caller **must initialize** the variable before passing it (`int x = 10;`).\n   - The called method is **not required** to modify it, though it usually reads and updates it.\n2. **`out` (One-Way: Output Only):**\n   - The caller does **not** need to initialize the variable before passing it (and can declare it inline: `int.TryParse(\"123\", out int result)`).\n   - The called method **must assign a value** to the `out` parameter before returning, or the code won't compile.\n3. **`in` (One-Way: Read-Only Reference, C# 7.2+):**\n   - The caller **must initialize** the variable before passing it.\n   - The called method **cannot modify** the parameter (compiler enforces read-only).\n   - **Why use `in`?** It is used for passing large `struct`s by reference to avoid the CPU overhead of copying 64+ bytes on the stack while guaranteeing the method won't mutate our data.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Rule | `ref` | `out` | `in` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Must be initialized by caller before passing?** | **Yes** | No | **Yes** |\n| **Must be assigned inside the method before returning?** | No | **Yes** | No (Cannot be modified!) |\n| **Can the method read the incoming value?** | Yes | No (treated as unassigned initially) | Yes |\n| **Real-World Use Case** | Swapping values, updating state | `int.TryParse`, `Dictionary.TryGetValue` | High-performance read-only large `struct`s |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class ParameterModifiersDemo\n{\n    // 1. REF: Caller initializes it; method can read and modify it\n    public void ApplyBonus(ref decimal salary)\n    {\n        salary += 5000m; // Optional to modify, but changes caller's variable directly\n    }\n\n    // 2. OUT: Caller doesn't need to initialize; method MUST assign values before returning\n    public bool TryDivide(int numerator, int denominator, out int quotient, out int remainder)\n    {\n        if (denominator == 0)\n        {\n            quotient = 0;\n            remainder = 0;\n            return false;\n        }\n        quotient = numerator / denominator;\n        remainder = numerator % denominator;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    // 3. IN: Caller initializes; method can READ by reference without copying, but CANNOT modify\n    public void LogTransactionId(in Guid transactionId)\n    {\n        // transactionId = Guid.NewGuid(); // ❌ Compiler Error: Cannot assign to 'in' variable!\n        Console.WriteLine($\"Tx: {transactionId}\");\n    }\n\n    public void RunExamples()\n    {\n        decimal mySalary = 50000m;\n        ApplyBonus(ref mySalary); // mySalary is now 55000m\n\n        // Inline out declaration (C# 7+)\n        if (TryDivide(17, 5, out int q, out int r))\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"17 / 5 = {q}, Remainder = {r}\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q4: Can two overloaded methods differ ONLY by `ref` and `out`?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**No!** A class cannot have `void Calculate(ref int x)` and `void Calculate(out int x)` in the same scope because both compile down to the exact same IL signature (`int32&` pointer). However, overloading `void Calculate(int x)` and `void Calculate(ref int x)` **is** allowed.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Under the hood, the CLR does not distinguish between `ref`, `out`, and `in` at the native pointer level—all three compile to a managed reference (`int32&`) with metadata attributes. Therefore, you **cannot** overload two methods that differ only by `ref`, `out`, or `in`. You **can**, however, overload a normal pass-by-value method (`void Process(int x)`) with a pass-by-reference method (`void Process(ref int x)`).\"\n\n---\n\n## Q5: What is the difference between `var`, `dynamic`, and `object` in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- `var` is **compile-time implicitly typed** (strongly typed; compiler figures out the exact type right away).\n- `dynamic` is **run-time typed** (bypasses compile-time checking; errors happen at runtime).\n- `object` is the **universal base class** (requires explicit casting and causes boxing for value types).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Developers sometimes confuse these three, so I always clarify when type checking happens:\n1. **`var` (Early Bound / Compile-Time Type Inference, C# 3.0):**\n   - `var` is **100% strongly typed** at compile time. It is purely syntactic sugar. When you write `var count = 10;`, the C# compiler replaces `var` with `int` in the compiled IL.\n   - Once assigned, you cannot change its type (`count = \"hello\"` gives a compile-time error).\n   - You get full IntelliSense, and `var` can only be used for **local variables** initialized on the same line (not class fields, method parameters, or return types).\n2. **`dynamic` (Late Bound / Run-Time Type Resolution, C# 4.0):**\n   - `dynamic` tells the C# compiler to **skip all compile-time type checking** and resolve methods/properties at **run time** using the DLR (Dynamic Language Runtime).\n   - You can assign an `int` to a `dynamic` variable on line 1 and a `string` on line 2.\n   - However, you lose IntelliSense, and if you call a non-existent method (`d.NonExistentMethod()`), it compiles fine and crashes at runtime with a `RuntimeBinderException`.\n   - *Where used:* Interacting with COM objects (Excel automation), dynamic JSON payloads, or Dapper's `connection.Query(\"SELECT ...\")`.\n3. **`object` (`System.Object`):**\n   - The root base class of all types in .NET.\n   - Can hold any value, but the compiler only lets you call basic `Object` methods (`.ToString()`, `.GetHashCode()`). To do math or call custom methods, you must **explicitly cast** it back, and storing value types in `object` causes **boxing**.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `var` | `dynamic` | `object` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type Checking** | **Compile-time** (Static) | **Run-time** (Dynamic via DLR) | **Compile-time** (Requires explicit cast) |\n| **Can change type later?** | **No** (`var x = 10; x = \"hi\";` ❌) | **Yes** (`dynamic x = 10; x = \"hi\";` ✅) | **Yes** (`object x = 10; x = \"hi\";` ✅) |\n| **IntelliSense Support** | Full IntelliSense | None | Only `System.Object` members |\n| **Allowed as Method Param / Return?** | **No** (Local variables only) | **Yes** | **Yes** |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class VarDynamicObjectDemo\n{\n    public void CompareAllThree()\n    {\n        // 1. VAR: Compiler immediately knows 'name' is System.String\n        var name = \"Karthik\";\n        int len1 = name.Length; // Full IntelliSense, compile-time safe!\n        // name = 123;          // ❌ Compiler Error!\n\n        // 2. DYNAMIC: Checked at runtime!\n        dynamic dynValue = \"Karthik\";\n        int len2 = dynValue.Length; // Resolved at runtime -> 7\n        dynValue = 500;             // ✅ Allowed to change type to int at runtime!\n        // dynValue.FakeMethod();   // ⚠️ Compiles fine, throws RuntimeBinderException at runtime!\n\n        // 3. OBJECT: Requires explicit casting to access string properties\n        object objValue = \"Karthik\";\n        // int len3 = objValue.Length; // ❌ Compiler Error: 'object' does not contain 'Length'\n        int len3 = ((string)objValue).Length; // Must explicitly cast!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q6: What is a `static` class in C#? What are its rules and when do we use it?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `static` class is a class that **cannot be instantiated** (`new` is not allowed) and **cannot be inherited**; it can contain only `static` members and lives in memory for the entire lifetime of the application.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When we mark a class as `static`, the C# compiler enforces several strict rules:\n1. You **cannot create an instance** of it using the `new` keyword.\n2. All members inside it (fields, properties, methods) **must also be `static`**.\n3. A `static` class is **implicitly `sealed` and `abstract`** at the IL level—meaning no class can inherit from it, and it cannot inherit from any class (except `System.Object`) or implement interfaces.\n4. It cannot have instance constructors, only a single parameterless `static` constructor.\n\n**When to use it in Real Projects:**\n- **Extension Methods:** Every extension method in C# (like `services.AddApplicationServices()` in `Program.cs` or custom LINQ extensions) **must** be defined inside a `static` class.\n- **Stateless Utility / Helper Methods:** Like `System.Math`, `System.Convert`, or pure mapping/formatting helpers that do not maintain any state.\n- **Constants & Configuration Keys:** Grouping application-wide `const` strings (like Role names or Cache keys).\n\n*(Caution for Senior Interviews)*: Avoid putting business logic or database calls in `static` classes because `static` method calls cannot be mocked easily in unit tests and cannot use Scoped Dependency Injection!\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\n// Extension methods MUST be in a non-nested, non-generic static class\npublic static class StringExtensions\n{\n    public static string MaskMobileNumber(this string mobile)\n    {\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(mobile) || mobile.Length < 4)\n            return mobile;\n\n        return new string('*', mobile.Length - 4) + mobile[^4..];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q7: What is a `static` constructor? When does it execute, and why is it parameterless and without access modifiers?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `static` constructor initializes static data of a class and is invoked **automatically by the CLR at most once**—right before the first instance is created or any static member is referenced. Because the CLR calls it (not us), it cannot have parameters or access modifiers.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"A `static` constructor (`static ClassName()`) is used to perform one-time initialization for a class (whether it's a `static` class or a normal class with `static` fields):\n1. **When does it run?** It is triggered automatically by the CLR **exactly once** per `AppDomain` lifetime, right before the first instance of the class is created **or** before any `static` member of the class is accessed—whichever happens first.\n2. **Why does it have no access modifiers (`public`/`private`)?** Because the developer never calls a static constructor directly in code—the **CLR invokes it automatically**, so `public` or `private` is meaningless.\n3. **Why is it parameterless?** Again, because the CLR triggers it automatically behind the scenes, there is no way for a developer to pass arguments to it.\n4. **Thread Safety:** The CLR guarantees that a static constructor is executed in a **thread-safe** manner (it places a lock around type initialization so two threads cannot run the static constructor twice).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic class AppConfigLoader\n{\n    public static readonly string EnvironmentName;\n\n    // Static Constructor: No 'public'/'private', no parameters!\n    static AppConfigLoader()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(\"1. Static Constructor called ONCE by CLR.\");\n        EnvironmentName = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT\") ?? \"Production\";\n    }\n\n    // Instance Constructor\n    public AppConfigLoader()\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(\"2. Instance Constructor called on every 'new'.\");\n    }\n}\n\n/*\nExecution Output if we run:\nvar loader1 = new AppConfigLoader();\nvar loader2 = new AppConfigLoader();\n\nOutput:\n1. Static Constructor called ONCE by CLR.\n2. Instance Constructor called on every 'new'.\n2. Instance Constructor called on every 'new'.\n*/\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q8: Can a `static` method access non-static (instance) members? What about the reverse?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `static` method **cannot** directly access instance (non-static) fields or methods without creating an object instance first, whereas an **instance method CAN** freely access all `static` fields and methods.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **Why `static` cannot access instance members:** A `static` method belongs to the class blueprint itself and can be called even when **zero objects** of that class exist in memory (`MyClass.StaticMethod()`). It has no `this` pointer. If 50 instances of `Employee` exist, which instance's `Salary` would a static method read? It wouldn't know unless you pass an `Employee` instance into it.\n- **Why instance methods CAN access `static` members:** An instance method runs on a specific object, and since `static` members are shared globally across the entire class in the High-Frequency Heap, any object instance can read or call them.\"\n\n---\n\n## Q9: What is a `sealed` class and a `sealed` method in C#? Why would you seal a class?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `sealed` class **cannot be inherited** by any other class, and a `sealed override` method prevents any further derived class from overriding that specific virtual method.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"1. **`sealed` Class:** When we mark a class as `sealed` (like `System.String`), no other class can inherit from it.\n   - **Design & Security Reason:** If a class handles sensitive security logic (like token generation or encryption) or has strict internal state invariants (like `string` immutability), sealing it prevents a subclass from overriding behavior and breaking security.\n   - **Performance Reason (.NET 6/7/8+ JIT Devirtualization):** When a class is `sealed`, the JIT compiler knows for certain that no child class can exist. This allows the JIT to **devirtualize** virtual/interface method calls and even inline them, making method calls faster!\n2. **`sealed` Method:** You cannot mark a brand-new method as `sealed` by itself. You can only use `sealed` together with `override` (`public sealed override void CalculateTax()`) inside a derived class. This allows your class to remain inheritable while locking down that one specific method so grandchildren classes cannot override it further.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\npublic class PaymentGateway\n{\n    public virtual void ValidateSignature() { /* Base validation */ }\n}\n\npublic class RazorpayGateway : PaymentGateway\n{\n    // Lock down signature validation so no child class of RazorpayGateway can tamper with it!\n    public sealed override void ValidateSignature()\n    {\n        // Strict HMAC-SHA256 verification\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CustomRazorpayGateway : RazorpayGateway\n{\n    // ❌ Compiler Error: cannot override inherited member 'ValidateSignature()' because it is sealed!\n    // public override void ValidateSignature() { }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q10: What is a `partial` class and `partial` method? Where have you seen them in real .NET projects?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `partial` class splits the definition of a single class, struct, or interface across two or more `.cs` files, which the C# compiler merges into one single class at compile time.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When we mark a class with the `partial` keyword across multiple files in the same assembly and namespace, the C# compiler combines all fields, properties, methods, and implemented interfaces into **one unified class** during compilation.\n\n**Where we use `partial` classes in real .NET projects:**\n1. **Entity Framework Core (Database-First / Scaffolding):** When EF Core scaffolds entity classes from a database (`Order.cs`), if we add custom business methods or `[NotMapped]` properties directly into that file, our code gets wiped out the next time someone re-scaffolds! Because EF generates `public partial class Order`, we create a separate `Order.Custom.cs` file with `public partial class Order` to safely add our custom logic.\n2. **WinForms / WPF / Blazor / Razor Pages (`Form1.cs` + `Form1.Designer.cs`):** The UI designer puts auto-generated control initialization code in `Form1.Designer.cs` while the developer writes event handlers in `Form1.cs`.\n3. **C# Source Generators (.NET 6/7/8):** High-performance `Regex` (`[GeneratedRegex]`) and `System.Text.Json` source generators require `partial` classes/methods so the compiler can generate code at build time.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\n// File 1: Customer.Generated.cs (Auto-generated by EF Core Scaffolding - DO NOT EDIT)\npublic partial class Customer\n{\n    public int Id { get; set; }\n    public string FirstName { get; set; }\n    public string LastName { get; set; }\n}\n\n// File 2: Customer.Custom.cs (Written by Developer - Safe from regeneration!)\npublic partial class Customer\n{\n    public string FullName => $\"{FirstName} {LastName}\";\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q11: What is the difference between a `struct` and a `class` in C#? When should you create a `struct`?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA `class` is a **Reference Type** stored on the Heap that supports inheritance, whereas a `struct` is a **Value Type** stored on the Stack (or inline) that copies by value and **does not support class inheritance**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Here is the complete breakdown between `struct` and `class`:\n1. **Type & Memory:** `class` is a Reference Type allocated on the Managed Heap (garbage-collected). `struct` is a Value Type allocated on the Stack or inline inside containing types (no GC overhead unless boxed).\n2. **Assignment Behavior:** Assigning a `class` copies only the reference address. Assigning a `struct` copies the **entire data value**.\n3. **Inheritance:** A `class` supports single inheritance from another class and can be `abstract` or `sealed`. A `struct` is implicitly `sealed`—it **cannot inherit** from another `struct` or `class`, and no class can inherit from it. However, a `struct` **can implement interfaces** (`IComparable<T>`, `IEquatable<T>`).\n4. **`null` Assignment:** A `class` variable can be `null` by default. A `struct` cannot be `null` unless wrapped in `Nullable<T>` (`MyStruct?`).\n\n**Microsoft's Rule of Thumb for choosing a `struct`:**\nOnly create a `struct` if:\n- It logically represents a single, small primitive-like value (like `Point`, `Money`, `DateRange`, `Guid`, `DateTime`).\n- Its instance size is small (**16 bytes or less**—otherwise copying it on every method call hurts performance).\n- It is **immutable** (`readonly struct`) and won't be boxed frequently.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `class` | `struct` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Category** | Reference Type | Value Type |\n| **Memory Allocation** | Managed Heap (GC cleaned) | Stack or Inline (Zero GC pressure) |\n| **Assignment (`b = a`)** | Copies memory reference only | Copies all field values |\n| **Inheritance** | Supports class & interface inheritance | Supports **interface** inheritance only (No class/struct inheritance) |\n| **Best For** | Entities, Services, Controllers, large objects | Small (<16 bytes), immutable values (`Money`, `Coordinate`) |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\n// Good use of readonly struct: Small (16 bytes), immutable value representing Money\npublic readonly struct Money : IEquatable<Money>\n{\n    public decimal Amount { get; }\n    public string Currency { get; }\n\n    public Money(decimal amount, string currency)\n    {\n        Amount = amount;\n        Currency = currency;\n    }\n\n    public bool Equals(Money other) => Amount == other.Amount && Currency == other.Currency;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q12: Explain all 6 Access Modifiers in C# (`public`, `private`, `protected`, `internal`, `protected internal`, `private protected`).\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nAccess modifiers control encapsulation (visibility of classes and members) across the **same class**, **derived (child) classes**, the **current Assembly (`.dll`/project)**, and **external Assemblies**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In C#, there are 6 access modifiers, and understanding how they behave across **Project/Assembly boundaries** is key:\n1. **`private`:** Accessible **only within the exact same class/struct**. (This is the default for class fields and methods if you don't write any modifier).\n2. **`protected`:** Accessible within the same class **and** inside any **derived (child) class**, even if that child class is in a different project/assembly.\n3. **`internal`:** Accessible **anywhere inside the same Assembly (`.dll` / C# Project)**, but hidden from other projects. (This is the default for top-level classes if you don't write `public class`).\n4. **`protected internal` (OR logic: `protected` OR `internal`):** Accessible anywhere in the same assembly **OR** in any derived class in an external assembly.\n5. **`private protected` (AND logic: `protected` AND `internal`, C# 7.2+):** Accessible **only within derived classes that are inside the exact same assembly**. External assemblies cannot access it even if they inherit the class!\n6. **`public`:** Accessible everywhere with no restrictions.\"\n\n### Visibility Matrix (Interview Cheat Sheet)\n| Access Modifier | Same Class | Derived Class (Same Assembly) | Non-Derived Class (Same Assembly) | Derived Class (External Assembly) | Non-Derived Class (External Assembly) |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **`private`** | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\n| **`private protected`** | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |\n| **`protected`** | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |\n| **`internal`** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |\n| **`protected internal`** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |\n| **`public`** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |\n\n> **Default Access Modifiers to Remember:**\n> - Top-level `class` / `interface` / `struct` / `enum` inside a namespace $\\rightarrow$ **`internal`**\n> - Class or Struct members (fields, properties, methods, constructors) $\\rightarrow$ **`private`**\n> - Interface members and Enum members $\\rightarrow$ **`public`**\n";

window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS["csharp-03"] = "# Lesson 03: Collections, Generics, LINQ & `IEnumerable<T>` vs `IQueryable<T>`\n\n> **Target Audience:** 5-Year Full Stack .NET Developer (ECE Background, 5+ Real Projects)\n> **Goal:** Ace the #1 5-year .NET interview topic: Collection performance, Generics, LINQ execution, and `IEnumerable<T>` vs `IQueryable<T>`.\n\n---\n\n## Q1: What is the difference between an `Array`, `ArrayList`, and `List<T>`? Why should we never use `ArrayList` in modern C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- **Array (`int[]`):** Fixed-size, strongly-typed contiguous memory block.\n- **`ArrayList`:** Legacy dynamic-size collection that stores everything as `object` (unsafe and causes boxing).\n- **`List<T>`:** Modern dynamic-size, strongly-typed generic collection that combines the safety/speed of an Array with auto-resizing.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"1. **Array (`int[]`):**\n   - **Pros:** Fastest performance and lowest memory overhead because elements are stored contiguously in memory and are strongly typed.\n   - **Cons:** **Fixed size.** Once you create `new int[5]`, you cannot add a 6th element without manually allocating a new array and copying elements.\n2. **`ArrayList` (`System.Collections` - Legacy .NET 1.0):**\n   - **Pros:** Resizes dynamically when you `.Add()` items.\n   - **Cons (Why it is bad):** It stores every element as `System.Object`. This creates two major problems:\n     - **No Compile-Time Type Safety:** Someone can add an `int` (`10`) and a `string` (`\"Ten\"`) to the same `ArrayList`, causing an `InvalidCastException` at runtime when iterating.\n     - **Severe Boxing/Unboxing Overhead:** Every time you add a value type (`int`, `decimal`, `DateTime`), it gets boxed onto the Heap, and unboxed when read.\n3. **`List<T>` (`System.Collections.Generic`):**\n   - Internally wraps a strongly-typed array `T[]`. When capacity is reached, it automatically allocates a new array of **double the capacity** and copies the items over.\n   - Gives **100% compile-time type safety** and **zero boxing/unboxing**.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `Array` (`T[]`) | `ArrayList` | `List<T>` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Namespace** | `System` | `System.Collections` | `System.Collections.Generic` |\n| **Size** | **Fixed** at creation | Dynamic (Auto-resizes) | Dynamic (Auto-resizes, doubles capacity) |\n| **Type Safety** | Strongly typed (Compile-time) | **Not type-safe** (Stores `object`) | Strongly typed (Compile-time) |\n| **Boxing / Unboxing** | None | **Yes** (Heavy GC penalty for value types) | None |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class CollectionComparisonDemo\n{\n    public void Compare()\n    {\n        // 1. Array: Fixed size, fast, type-safe\n        int[] scores = new int[3] { 90, 85, 88 };\n\n        // 2. ❌ ArrayList: No type safety + Boxing penalty!\n        ArrayList legacyList = new ArrayList();\n        legacyList.Add(100);     // Boxed to object on Heap!\n        legacyList.Add(\"Hello\"); // Allowed at compile-time!\n        // int total = (int)legacyList[0] + (int)legacyList[1]; // 💥 Runtime InvalidCastException!\n\n        // 3. ✅ List<T>: Dynamic size + Compile-time type safety + Zero boxing!\n        List<int> modernList = new List<int>();\n        modernList.Add(100);\n        // modernList.Add(\"Hello\"); // ✅ Caught immediately at Compile-Time!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q2: What are Generics (`<T>`) in C#? How do they provide Type Safety, Code Reusability, and Performance?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nGenerics allow you to write a class, interface, or method with a **type placeholder (`<T>`)** so the exact data type is specified by the caller at compile time—giving you one reusable codebase with strict type safety and zero boxing.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Before Generics were introduced in C# 2.0, if we wanted to write a reusable `ApiResponse` wrapper or a `Repository` class that worked for `Order`, `Customer`, and `Product`, we had two bad options: either write duplicate classes for each entity, or use `object` (which lost type safety and caused boxing).\n\nGenerics solve this with **three major benefits**:\n1. **Code Reusability:** We write `GenericRepository<T>` or `ApiResponse<T>` once, and reuse it for `ApiResponse<OrderDto>`, `ApiResponse<List<UserDto>>`, or `ApiResponse<int>`.\n2. **Compile-Time Type Safety:** The compiler enforces that `List<Order>` only accepts `Order` objects, catching bugs at build time instead of production runtime.\n3. **High Performance (No Boxing or Casting):** At runtime, the JIT compiler generates specialized native code for value types (`List<int>` uses an actual `int[]` in memory) and shares native code pointers for reference types, completely eliminating boxing and explicit casting.\"\n\n### Code Example (Real Project `ApiResponse<T>` Pattern)\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\n// Real Project Example: Standardized API Response Wrapper used across all Controllers\npublic class ApiResponse<T>\n{\n    public bool IsSuccess { get; set; }\n    public string Message { get; set; }\n    public T Data { get; set; } // <T> adapts to whatever DTO we return!\n\n    public static ApiResponse<T> Success(T data, string message = \"Success\")\n        => new ApiResponse<T> { IsSuccess = true, Data = data, Message = message };\n}\n\npublic class GenericConstraintExample<T> where T : class, new()\n{\n    // Generic Constraints ('where T : class, new()') ensure T is a Reference Type with a parameterless constructor\n    public T CreateDefaultInstance() => new T();\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q3: What is the difference between `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`, `Hashtable`, and `HashSet<T>`?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` stores **unique key-value pairs** generically with $O(1)$ lookup.\n- `Hashtable` is the legacy non-generic (`object`-based) version of `Dictionary`.\n- `HashSet<T>` stores **unique values only** (no key-value pair) for ultra-fast $O(1)$ duplicate checking and set operations.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"All three use **Hash Table buckets** under the hood (calling `.GetHashCode()` and `.Equals()`) to provide near **$O(1)$ constant-time lookup**, which is massively faster than searching a `List<T>` ($O(N)$ linear scan):\n1. **`Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`:**\n   - Generic, strongly-typed Key-Value collection.\n   - Keys must be unique and cannot be `null`.\n   - If you access a missing key via `dict[key]`, it throws a `KeyNotFoundException`—so in production code we always use `dict.TryGetValue(key, out var val)`.\n2. **`Hashtable`:**\n   - Legacy non-generic Key-Value collection (`object` keys and `object` values). Causes boxing/unboxing. (Note: If someone asks what happens when you access a missing key in `Hashtable`, it returns `null` instead of throwing `KeyNotFoundException`).\n3. **`HashSet<T>`:**\n   - Think of `HashSet<T>` as a `Dictionary` with **keys only and no values**.\n   - It automatically rejects duplicates (`set.Add(x)` returns `false` if `x` already exists) and provides $O(1)$ `.Contains(x)` checks.\n   - **Real Project Example:** When validating 10,000 uploaded Excel rows against existing Employee Codes, putting existing codes into a `HashSet<string>` makes `.Contains()` instantaneous ($O(1)$) compared to `list.Contains()` ($O(N)$).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class HashingCollectionsDemo\n{\n    public void RunDemo()\n    {\n        // 1. Dictionary<TKey, TValue>: Fast O(1) lookup by Key\n        var statusMap = new Dictionary<int, string>\n        {\n            { 1, \"Pending\" },\n            { 2, \"Approved\" }\n        };\n\n        // Safe lookup without throwing KeyNotFoundException\n        if (statusMap.TryGetValue(2, out string statusName))\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"Status: {statusName}\");\n        }\n\n        // 2. HashSet<T>: Fast O(1) uniqueness & duplicate elimination\n        var processedOrderIds = new HashSet<int>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase is null ? null : null);\n        bool firstAdd = processedOrderIds.Add(1001);  // Returns true\n        bool secondAdd = processedOrderIds.Add(1001); // Returns false (Duplicate ignored!)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q4: Compare `IEnumerable<T>`, `ICollection<T>`, and `IList<T>`. Which one should you return from a method?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nThey form an inheritance hierarchy of capabilities:\n- `IEnumerable<T>`: **Read-only forward iteration** (`foreach` only).\n- `ICollection<T>`: Adds **`.Count`, `.Add()`, `.Remove()`, `.Contains()`**.\n- `IList<T>`: Adds **index-based access (`list[i]`), `.InsertAt()`, `.RemoveAt()`**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"These three interfaces inherit from one another in a chain: `IList<T> : ICollection<T> : IEnumerable<T>`:\n1. **`IEnumerable<T>` (Lowest level / Least restrictive):**\n   - Only exposes `GetEnumerator()`.\n   - Allows forward-only iteration using `foreach` and LINQ queries.\n   - You **cannot** add/remove items, access by index `[0]`, or even get `.Count` without iterating through the sequence (unless the underlying type is cast).\n2. **`ICollection<T>` (Inherits `IEnumerable<T>`):**\n   - Adds the `.Count` property (in $O(1)$ time) and mutation methods: `.Add()`, `.Remove()`, `.Clear()`, and `.Contains()`.\n3. **`IList<T>` (Inherits `ICollection<T>`):**\n   - Adds random index access (`items[2]`) and positional methods like `.IndexOf()`, `.Insert(index, item)`, and `.RemoveAt(index)`.\n4. **Modern .NET Best Practice (`IReadOnlyList<T>` / `IReadOnlyCollection<T>`):**\n   - In our Clean Architecture services, if we return an in-memory collection to a caller and don't want the caller mutating our list, we return `IReadOnlyList<T>` or `IEnumerable<T>`.\"\n\n### Hierarchy Diagram\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ IEnumerable<T>                                           │\n│  • GetEnumerator() -> Forward-only foreach & LINQ        │\n└────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┘\n                             │ inherits\n                             ▼\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ ICollection<T>                                           │\n│  • .Count, .Add(T), .Remove(T), .Clear(), .Contains(T)   │\n└────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┘\n                             │ inherits\n                             ▼\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ IList<T>                                                 │\n│  • Indexer [i], .IndexOf(T), .Insert(i, T), .RemoveAt(i) │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q5: What is the difference between `IEnumerable<T>` and `IQueryable<T>`? (The #1 5-Year Interview Question!)\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`IEnumerable<T>` executes LINQ filters **in-memory on the application server** (fetching all rows from the DB first if misused!), whereas `IQueryable<T>` builds an **Expression Tree** that EF Core translates into a **SQL `WHERE` query executed directly inside the Database**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"This is the most critical distinction when working with Entity Framework Core and large databases:\n1. **Where the Filter Executes:**\n   - **`IEnumerable<T>` (`System.Collections.Generic`):** Designed for querying **in-memory collections** (LINQ-to-Objects). Its `.Where()` extension method accepts a compiled **Delegate (`Func<T, bool>`)**. If you assign a `DbSet<Order>` to an `IEnumerable<Order>` and then apply `.Where(x => x.IsActive).Take(10)`, EF Core **cannot** inspect inside a compiled `Func<>` delegate! So EF executes `SELECT * FROM Orders` (pulling **1 million rows** across the network into RAM) and filters them in C# memory!\n   - **`IQueryable<T>` (`System.Linq`):** Designed for querying **remote data providers** like SQL Server (LINQ-to-Entities / EF Core). It inherits from `IEnumerable<T>`, but its `.Where()` extension method accepts an **Expression Tree (`Expression<Func<T, bool>>`)**. EF Core inspects this Expression Tree and translates the entire chain of `.Where()`, `.OrderBy()`, and `.Take()` into a single optimized SQL query (`SELECT TOP(10) ... FROM Orders WHERE IsActive = 1`), returning only 10 rows over the network!\n2. **Real Project Bug Example:**\n   In one of our projects, a junior developer wrote a repository method `public IEnumerable<Invoice> GetAllInvoices() => _dbContext.Invoices;` and then filtered by `CustomerId` in the service layer. In production, the table grew to 500,000 rows and the API timed out because `IEnumerable` pulled the entire table into memory before filtering! Changing the query composition to use `IQueryable<Invoice>` before calling `.ToListAsync()` reduced the response time from 12 seconds to 18 milliseconds.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `IEnumerable<T>` | `IQueryable<T>` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Namespace** | `System.Collections.Generic` | `System.Linq` |\n| **LINQ Extension Parameter** | **`Func<T, bool>`** (Compiled IL Delegate) | **`Expression<Func<T, bool>>`** (Expression Tree AST) |\n| **Where Filtering Happens** | **Client-side / In-Memory** (App Server RAM) | **Server-side / Database Engine** (Translated to SQL) |\n| **Pagination (`Skip`/`Take`)** | Fetches all rows into RAM, then skips/takes | Translates to SQL `OFFSET ... FETCH NEXT` or `TOP(N)` |\n| **Best Used For** | In-memory `List<T>`, Arrays, already-fetched data | Entity Framework (`DbSet<T>`), remote database queries |\n\n### Exact Code Example (In-Memory vs SQL Generation)\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;\nusing Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;\n\npublic class OrderRepository\n{\n    private readonly AppDbContext _context;\n    public OrderRepository(AppDbContext context) => _context = context;\n\n    // ❌ BAD: Using IEnumerable<T> before filtering a database table!\n    public List<Order> GetHighValueOrders_Bad()\n    {\n        IEnumerable<Order> query = _context.Orders; // Upcast to IEnumerable!\n\n        // Uses Enumerable.Where(Func<Order, bool>)\n        // SQL Sent to DB: SELECT * FROM [Orders]  <-- FETCHES ENTIRE TABLE INTO RAM!\n        return query\n            .Where(o => o.TotalAmount > 10000)\n            .Take(5)\n            .ToList(); // Filters in C# memory after loading everything!\n    }\n\n    // ✅ GOOD: Using IQueryable<T> so EF Core translates Expression Tree to SQL!\n    public List<Order> GetHighValueOrders_Good()\n    {\n        IQueryable<Order> query = _context.Orders; // Stays IQueryable!\n\n        // Uses Queryable.Where(Expression<Func<Order, bool>>)\n        // SQL Sent to DB: SELECT TOP(5) * FROM [Orders] WHERE [TotalAmount] > 10000\n        return query\n            .Where(o => o.TotalAmount > 10000)\n            .Take(5)\n            .ToList(); // Only 5 matching rows travel over the network!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q6: What is Deferred Execution vs Immediate Execution in LINQ?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n**Deferred Execution** means a LINQ query is not executed when you declare it—it only stores the query plan and executes when you actually iterate over it (`foreach`) or call a materializing method. **Immediate Execution** forces the query to run right away and cache the results (e.g., `.ToList()`, `.Count()`).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **Deferred (Lazy) Execution:**\n  Operators like `.Where()`, `.Select()`, `.OrderBy()`, `.Skip()`, and `.Take()` use **deferred execution**. Writing `var activeUsers = users.Where(u => u.IsActive);` does **zero work** and hits **zero database tables** on that line. It simply constructs an iterator state machine (or SQL Expression Tree).\n  - **Why is this powerful?** It allows us to dynamically chain filters based on `if` conditions (`if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(search)) query = query.Where(...);`) before sending a single final SQL query to the database.\n  - **What is the trap (Multiple Enumeration)?** Because a deferred query has no stored result, if you loop over `activeUsers` twice in two separate `foreach` loops, it will **execute the query (or hit the database) twice**!\n- **Immediate (Eager) Execution:**\n  Operators that return a concrete collection or a single scalar value—such as `.ToList()`, `.ToArray()`, `.ToDictionary()`, `.Count()`, `.First()`, `.FirstOrDefault()`, `.Any()`, `.Sum()`—execute the query **immediately** on that line and materialize the result in memory.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;\n\npublic class DeferredExecutionDemo\n{\n    public void ShowDeferredBehavior()\n    {\n        var numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3 };\n\n        // 1. DEFERRED: Query is defined, NOT executed yet!\n        var evenQuery = numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0);\n\n        // We add 4 to the list AFTER defining the query\n        numbers.Add(4);\n\n        // Query executes NOW during foreach -> includes 4!\n        foreach (var n in evenQuery)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine(n); // Prints: 2, 4\n        }\n\n        // 2. IMMEDIATE: .ToList() executes immediately and freezes the snapshot in a new List\n        var frozenEvens = numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0).ToList();\n        numbers.Add(6); // Adding 6 now does NOT affect frozenEvens!\n        Console.WriteLine(frozenEvens.Count); // Output: 2 (Still only 2 and 4)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q7: What is the difference between `First()`, `FirstOrDefault()`, `Single()`, and `SingleOrDefault()` in LINQ?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- **`First` / `FirstOrDefault`** returns the **first matching element** even if multiple matches exist (`TOP 1` in SQL).\n- **`Single` / `SingleOrDefault`** enforces **strictly at most one match**—if 2 or more matches exist, both throw an exception (`TOP 2` in SQL).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Interviewers love asking how these four behave across **three scenarios** (0 records found, 1 record found, >1 records found) and what SQL they generate:\n1. **`First()`:** Expects **at least 1** match. Returns the first element. Throws `InvalidOperationException` if the sequence is empty.\n2. **`FirstOrDefault()`:** Expects **0 or more** matches. Returns the first element, or `default(T)` (`null` for classes, `0` for `int`) if empty. Never throws on multiple matches. (Generates `SELECT TOP(1)` in EF Core).\n3. **`Single()`:** Expects **1 and only 1** match. Throws `InvalidOperationException` if empty **AND** throws `InvalidOperationException` if **more than 1** element matches!\n4. **`SingleOrDefault()`:** Expects **0 or 1** match. Returns `default(T)` if empty, returns the element if 1 match, and **throws `InvalidOperationException` if more than 1 element matches**!\n\n**5-Year Performance Tip for EF Core:**\nWhy does `SingleOrDefault()` generate `SELECT TOP(2)` in SQL instead of `SELECT TOP(1)`? Because EF Core must check if a **second** matching row exists in the database so it knows whether to throw the 'Sequence contains more than one element' exception! Also, on an unindexed table in memory, `FirstOrDefault()` stops scanning as soon as it finds the first match, whereas `SingleOrDefault()` must scan the **entire collection** to verify no duplicate exists.\"\n\n### Behavior Matrix (Memorize This!)\n| LINQ Method | 0 Elements Found | 1 Element Found | >1 Elements Found (Duplicates) | SQL Generated (EF Core) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`First()`** | 💥 Throws `InvalidOperationException` | ✅ Returns 1st | ✅ Returns 1st (Stops scanning) | `SELECT TOP(1) ...` |\n| **`FirstOrDefault()`** | ✅ Returns `default(T)` (`null` / `0`) | ✅ Returns 1st | ✅ Returns 1st (Stops scanning) | `SELECT TOP(1) ...` |\n| **`Single()`** | 💥 Throws `InvalidOperationException` | ✅ Returns the 1 | 💥 Throws `InvalidOperationException` | `SELECT TOP(2) ...` |\n| **`SingleOrDefault()`** | ✅ Returns `default(T)` (`null` / `0`) | ✅ Returns the 1 | 💥 Throws `InvalidOperationException` | `SELECT TOP(2) ...` |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;\n\npublic class FirstVsSingleDemo\n{\n    public void TestMethods()\n    {\n        var roles = new List<string> { \"Admin\", \"User\", \"User\" };\n\n        // 1 match: Both work fine\n        string admin = roles.SingleOrDefault(r => r == \"Admin\"); // \"Admin\"\n\n        // 0 matches: OrDefault returns null safely\n        string guest = roles.FirstOrDefault(r => r == \"Guest\");  // null\n        string guest2 = roles.SingleOrDefault(r => r == \"Guest\"); // null\n\n        // 2 matches (\"User\"):\n        string firstUser = roles.FirstOrDefault(r => r == \"User\"); // ✅ Returns first \"User\"\n        // string singleUser = roles.SingleOrDefault(r => r == \"User\"); // 💥 THROWS InvalidOperationException (\"More than one element\")!\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q8: What is the difference between `Select` and `SelectMany` in LINQ?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`Select` performs a **1-to-1 projection** (returning a list of lists if you select a nested collection), whereas `SelectMany` **flattens** a 1-to-Many nested collection into a **single flat sequence**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Suppose we have a list of `Department` objects, and each `Department` has a `List<Employee> Employees`:\n- **`Select` (1-to-1 Mapping):** If we write `departments.Select(d => d.Employees)`, since there are 4 departments, `Select` returns `IEnumerable<List<Employee>>`—a nested collection of 4 lists. To iterate over every employee, we would need two nested `foreach` loops.\n- **`SelectMany` (1-to-Many Flattening):** If we write `departments.SelectMany(d => d.Employees)`, it **flattens** all the inner `List<Employee>` collections into a single flat `IEnumerable<Employee>` stream! In EF Core, `SelectMany` translates to an `INNER JOIN` or `CROSS APPLY` in SQL.\"\n\n### Visual Diagram\n```\nInput: 2 Departments, each with 2 Employees\n  Dept A: [ Emp1, Emp2 ]\n  Dept B: [ Emp3, Emp4 ]\n\ndepts.Select(d => d.Employees)     ──► [ [Emp1, Emp2], [Emp3, Emp4] ]  (IEnumerable<List<Employee>>)\ndepts.SelectMany(d => d.Employees) ──► [ Emp1, Emp2, Emp3, Emp4 ]      (IEnumerable<Employee> - Flattened!)\n```\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;\n\npublic class Department\n{\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n    public List<string> SkillsRequired { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class SelectManyDemo\n{\n    public void FlattenSkills(List<Department> departments)\n    {\n        // ❌ Select: Returns IEnumerable<List<string>> (Nested!)\n        IEnumerable<List<string>> nestedSkills = departments.Select(d => d.SkillsRequired);\n\n        // ✅ SelectMany: Flattens all lists into a single IEnumerable<string> and removes duplicates!\n        List<string> allUniqueSkills = departments\n            .SelectMany(d => d.SkillsRequired)\n            .Distinct()\n            .ToList();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q9: Why should you use `.Any()` instead of `.Count() > 0` to check if a collection or database query has items?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`.Any()` stops immediately as soon as it finds the **very first matching item** ($O(1)$ / `IF EXISTS` in SQL), whereas `.Count() > 0` scans and counts **every single matching record** ($O(N)$ / `SELECT COUNT(*)` in SQL) just to check if at least one exists.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"This is a classic code-review performance optimization:\n- When you call `orders.Where(o => o.Status == \"Pending\").Count() > 0`, LINQ has to iterate through the **entire** sequence (or run a full `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders WHERE Status = 'Pending'` scan in SQL) even if there are 500,000 pending orders, just to tell you that the count is greater than zero!\n- When you call `orders.Any(o => o.Status == \"Pending\")`, LINQ checks only the **first matching row** and immediately short-circuits and returns `true` (generating `IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM Orders WHERE ...)` in SQL).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Linq;\n\npublic class AnyVsCountDemo\n{\n    public bool HasPendingOrders(IQueryable<Order> ordersQuery)\n    {\n        // ❌ BAD: Counts all 500,000 matching rows in SQL!\n        // bool hasPending = ordersQuery.Where(o => o.IsPending).Count() > 0;\n\n        // ✅ GOOD: Stops at the 1st matching row (SQL EXISTS)!\n        return ordersQuery.Any(o => o.IsPending);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q10: How does the `yield return` keyword work in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`yield return` allows a method that returns `IEnumerable<T>` to produce elements **lazily one at a time on demand** without allocating a temporary `List<T>` in memory.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Normally, if a method returns `IEnumerable<int>`, we create a `new List<int>()`, populate all items inside a loop, and return the list.\nWhen we use `yield return` inside an iterator method:\n1. The C# compiler automatically generates a hidden **state machine class** behind the scenes.\n2. Calling the method does not execute a single line of its body immediately (Deferred Execution).\n3. Each time the caller's `foreach` loop asks for the next item (`MoveNext()`), the method executes up to the next `yield return`, returns that single item, and **pauses/remembers its exact position** and local variables until the next iteration!\n4. `yield break` can be used to stop iteration early.\n\n**Real Project Use Case:** Streaming and transforming 100,000 lines from a CSV file one row at a time with constant $O(1)$ memory usage instead of loading all 100,000 objects into a `List<T>` at once.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.IO;\n\npublic class LogStreamer\n{\n    // Streams only ERROR lines from a huge 2GB log file WITHOUT loading the whole file into a List!\n    public IEnumerable<string> ReadErrorLinesLazily(string filePath)\n    {\n        using var reader = new StreamReader(filePath);\n        string line;\n        while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)\n        {\n            if (line.StartsWith(\"[ERROR]\"))\n            {\n                // Returns 1 line at a time to the caller's foreach loop, then pauses!\n                yield return line;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q11: What are `ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>` and thread-safe collections? Why does a regular `List<T>` or `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` fail in multi-threaded code?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nStandard collections like `List<T>` and `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` are **not thread-safe**—if two threads add/modify items simultaneously or one modifies while another iterates, they corrupt internal state or throw `InvalidOperationException`. For multi-threaded shared state (like a Singleton cache), we use `System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>`.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In ASP.NET Core, if we register a service as a **Singleton** and store an in-memory `Dictionary<string, TokenInfo>` inside it, multiple HTTP request threads will hit that dictionary at the exact same millisecond.\n- A standard `Dictionary` is not thread-safe for concurrent writes—it can enter an infinite loop, corrupt internal hash buckets, or throw `InvalidOperationException: Collection was modified; enumeration operation may not execute`.\n- Instead of locking the entire dictionary with `lock(_syncRoot)` (which blocks all threads), we use **`ConcurrentDictionary<TKey, TValue>`** from `System.Collections.Concurrent`. It uses fine-grained lock striping and atomic methods like `.GetOrAdd(key, valueFactory)` and `.AddOrUpdate()` so multiple threads can read and write safely and concurrently.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Concurrent;\n\npublic class TenantCacheService // Registered as Singleton in DI\n{\n    // Thread-safe dictionary for concurrent HTTP requests\n    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> _tenantConnections = new();\n\n    public string GetOrLoadConnectionString(string tenantId)\n    {\n        // Thread-safe atomic lookup-or-create\n        return _tenantConnections.GetOrAdd(tenantId, id => $\"Server=db-{id};Database=AppDb;\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q12: How do `GroupBy`, `Join`, and `GroupJoin` (Left Join) work in LINQ?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n- `GroupBy` groups elements by a shared key (like SQL `GROUP BY`) into `IGrouping<TKey, TElement>` buckets.\n- `Join` performs an **Inner Join** between two sequences.\n- `GroupJoin` combined with `SelectMany` and `.DefaultIfEmpty()` performs a **Left Outer Join**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In real-world reporting APIs, we frequently aggregate data using `GroupBy`:\n- When we call `.GroupBy(o => o.DepartmentId)`, it returns groups where each group has a `.Key` (the `DepartmentId`) and is itself an `IEnumerable` of all items in that group, allowing us to project `.Count()`, `.Sum()`, and `.Average()`.\n- In Entity Framework Core, if navigation properties are configured (`order.Customer`), we rarely need manual `.Join()` because EF automatically generates `INNER JOIN` or `LEFT JOIN` when we reference navigation properties in `.Select()`. However, for unmapped tables or in-memory lists, `Join` does an Inner Join, and `GroupJoin` + `DefaultIfEmpty()` does a Left Join.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Linq;\n\npublic class SalesSummaryDto\n{\n    public string Category { get; set; }\n    public int TotalOrders { get; set; }\n    public decimal TotalRevenue { get; set; }\n}\n\npublic class LinqAggregationDemo\n{\n    public List<SalesSummaryDto> GetCategoryWiseSales(IQueryable<Order> orders)\n    {\n        // Translates directly to:\n        // SELECT Category, COUNT(*), SUM(TotalAmount) FROM Orders GROUP BY Category\n        return orders\n            .GroupBy(o => o.Category)\n            .Select(g => new SalesSummaryDto\n            {\n                Category = g.Key,\n                TotalOrders = g.Count(),\n                TotalRevenue = g.Sum(x => x.TotalAmount)\n            })\n            .OrderByDescending(dto => dto.TotalRevenue)\n            .ToList();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Order\n{\n    public string Category { get; set; }\n    public decimal TotalAmount { get; set; }\n    public bool IsPending { get; set; }\n}\npublic class AppDbContext : Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.DbContext\n{\n    public Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.DbSet<Order> Orders { get; set; }\n}\n```\n";

window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS["csharp-04"] = "# Lesson 04: Delegates, `async`/`await`, Exception Handling & Garbage Collection\n\n> **Target Audience:** 5-Year Full Stack .NET Developer (ECE Background, 5+ Real Projects)\n> **Goal:** Master the advanced runtime topics required for 5-year senior roles: Delegates (`Func`/`Action`/`Predicate`), Asynchronous programming (`async`/`await`, `Task`), Exception handling (`throw` vs `throw ex`), and Garbage Collection (`Gen 0/1/2`, `IDisposable`).\n\n---\n\n## Q1: What is a Delegate in C#? Why are delegates called \"type-safe function pointers\"?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA **Delegate** is a Reference Type that holds a reference (pointer) to a method with a specific parameter and return-type signature, allowing you to pass methods as arguments to other methods (callbacks).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In C/C++, function pointers were raw memory addresses that were not type-safe. In C#, a **Delegate** is a **type-safe, object-oriented function pointer**:\n1. When you declare a delegate (e.g., `public delegate decimal DiscountStrategy(decimal price);`), the C# compiler generates a class inheriting from `System.MulticastDelegate`.\n2. It enforces strict **compile-time signature checking**: you can only point the delegate to a method whose parameter types and return type match the delegate's signature.\n3. **Why do we use Delegates?**\n   - **Callbacks & Event-Driven Programming:** Letting a background worker or UI button notify a caller when an event occurs.\n   - **LINQ & Functional Programming:** Passing filtering or sorting logic into methods (`.Where(x => x.Age > 18)`).\n   - **Decoupling Business Rules:** Passing a strategy method into a processor without hardcoding `if/else` blocks.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\n// 1. Declare the Delegate signature\npublic delegate decimal DiscountCalculator(decimal orderTotal);\n\npublic class BillingService\n{\n    // 2. Method accepts a Delegate as a parameter (Callback / Strategy)\n    public decimal GenerateFinalBill(decimal orderTotal, DiscountCalculator discountStrategy)\n    {\n        decimal discount = discountStrategy(orderTotal); // Invokes the passed-in method!\n        return orderTotal - discount;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class DelegateDemo\n{\n    public static decimal FestivalDiscount(decimal total) => total * 0.20m;\n    public static decimal VipDiscount(decimal total) => total * 0.30m;\n\n    public void Run()\n    {\n        var billing = new BillingService();\n        // Pass method 'FestivalDiscount' as an argument!\n        decimal finalAmount = billing.GenerateFinalBill(1000m, FestivalDiscount); // Returns 800m\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q2: What is a Multicast Delegate? What happens if a Multicast Delegate has a return value or throws an exception?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA **Multicast Delegate** holds references to **multiple methods** in an invocation list (combined using `+=`), calling them sequentially in order when invoked.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Every delegate in C# inherits from `System.MulticastDelegate`, which means a single delegate instance can point to multiple methods using the `+=` operator (and unsubscribe using `-=`):\n1. **Sequential Execution:** When invoked, it executes each subscribed method in the exact order they were added.\n2. **Interview Trap #1 — Return Values:** If a multicast delegate has a non-void return type (e.g., `int`), all subscribed methods still execute, but the caller receives **only the return value of the very last method** in the invocation list! Previous return values are overwritten unless you manually iterate through `del.GetInvocationList()`.\n3. **Interview Trap #2 — Exceptions:** If the 1st method in a 3-method multicast delegate throws an unhandled exception, execution stops immediately and the **2nd and 3rd methods are never called**! Therefore, multicast delegates are almost always used with `void` methods (such as Event notifications: Email + SMS + Audit Log).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\npublic delegate void OrderNotificationHandler(int orderId);\n\npublic class NotificationDemo\n{\n    public void SendEmail(int id) => Console.WriteLine($\"Email sent for Order #{id}\");\n    public void SendSms(int id)   => Console.WriteLine($\"SMS sent for Order #{id}\");\n    public void WriteAudit(int id) => Console.WriteLine($\"Audit logged for Order #{id}\");\n\n    public void TriggerOrderPlaced(int orderId)\n    {\n        // Combine multiple methods into a single Multicast Delegate using +=\n        OrderNotificationHandler notifier = SendEmail;\n        notifier += SendSms;\n        notifier += WriteAudit;\n\n        // Invokes all 3 methods in sequence!\n        notifier(orderId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q3: What is the difference between `Func<>`, `Action<>`, and `Predicate<>`?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nThey are built-in generic delegates provided by .NET so you don't have to declare custom `delegate` types:\n- **`Func<T, TResult>`** takes 0 to 16 inputs and **always returns a value** (the last type parameter is the return type).\n- **`Action<T>`** takes 0 to 16 inputs and **returns `void` (nothing)**.\n- **`Predicate<T>`** takes **1 input** and always returns **`bool`**.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In modern C#, we rarely write `public delegate ...` manually because .NET provides three built-in generic delegates in the `System` namespace:\n1. **`Func<in T1, ..., out TResult>`:**\n   - Used for any method that **returns a value**.\n   - Can take `0` to `16` input parameters, and the **last type parameter is always the return type**. For example, `Func<int, int, string>` takes two `int` parameters and returns a `string`.\n   - *Where used:* Almost all LINQ methods use `Func<>`! `.Where(Func<T, bool>)`, `.Select(Func<T, TResult>)`.\n2. **`Action<in T1, ...>`:**\n   - Used for any method that performs an action and **returns `void`**.\n   - Can take `0` to `16` input parameters (`Action` with no `<T>` takes 0 parameters and returns `void`).\n   - *Where used:* `list.ForEach(Action<T>)`, configuring middleware/options in `Program.cs` (`services.AddControllers(options => ...)`).\n3. **`Predicate<in T>`:**\n   - A specialized delegate that takes **exactly 1 input parameter** of type `T` and always returns a **`bool`**.\n   - Equivalent to `Func<T, bool>`, and used in `List<T>` methods like `list.FindAll(Predicate<T>)` and `list.RemoveAll(Predicate<T>)`.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Built-in Delegate | Input Parameters | Return Type | Example Signature | Equivalent Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`Func<...>`** | 0 to 16 inputs | **Always returns `TResult`** (Last parameter) | `Func<int, bool>` | Takes `int`, returns `bool` |\n| **`Action<...>`** | 0 to 16 inputs | **`void`** (No return value) | `Action<string>` | Takes `string`, returns `void` |\n| **`Predicate<T>`** | **Strictly 1 input** | **Always `bool`** | `Predicate<Order>` | Takes `Order`, returns `bool` |\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\npublic class BuiltInDelegatesDemo\n{\n    public void RunExamples()\n    {\n        // 1. Func<int, int, int>: Takes two ints (a, b), returns an int (a + b)\n        Func<int, int, int> addNumbers = (a, b) => a + b;\n        int sum = addNumbers(10, 20); // 30\n\n        // 2. Action<string>: Takes a string, returns void\n        Action<string> logMessage = msg => Console.WriteLine($\"[INFO]: {msg}\");\n        logMessage($\"Sum calculated: {sum}\");\n\n        // 3. Predicate<int>: Takes one int, returns bool\n        Predicate<int> isAdult = age => age >= 18;\n        var ages = new List<int> { 12, 25, 17, 30 };\n        List<int> adults = ages.FindAll(isAdult); // [25, 30]\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q4: What are Anonymous Methods and Lambda Expressions (`=>`)?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nAn **Anonymous Method** (`delegate(int x) { return x > 5; }`) is an inline method without a name, and a **Lambda Expression** (`x => x > 5`) is the cleaner, concise syntax used for writing inline delegates and LINQ Expression Trees.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"Instead of creating a separate named method just to pass a one-line filter into a delegate, C# evolved through three stages:\n1. **C# 1.0 (Named Methods):** Had to write a separate method `bool IsActive(User u)` and pass it.\n2. **C# 2.0 (Anonymous Methods):** Introduced the `delegate` keyword inline: `users.Where(delegate(User u) { return u.IsActive; });`.\n3. **C# 3.0+ (Lambda Expressions `=>`):** Introduced the lambda operator `=>` (read as *'goes to'*): `users.Where(u => u.IsActive);`. The compiler automatically infers parameter types (`u` is inferred as `User`) and can compile a lambda into either executable IL code (`Func<T, bool>`) or an **Expression Tree** (`Expression<Func<T, bool>>`) for EF Core SQL translation.\"\n\n---\n\n## Q5: How do `async` and `await` work in C# without blocking threads?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nWhen an `async` method hits an `await` on an I/O operation (like a SQL query or HTTP API call), **it does NOT block the thread**—instead, it releases the thread back to the .NET ThreadPool to serve other HTTP requests, and resumes execution when the I/O finishes.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"This is one of the most important concepts for building scalable ASP.NET Core Web APIs:\n1. **The Synchronous Blocking Problem:** In a synchronous API call (`_dbContext.Orders.ToList()`), while SQL Server takes 50ms to execute the query and return data over the network, the ASP.NET Core worker thread sits **completely idle and blocked**, waiting. If 500 concurrent users hit the API, the ThreadPool runs out of threads (Thread Starvation) and requests start queuing or failing with `503 Service Unavailable`.\n2. **How `async` / `await` Solves This (Non-Blocking I/O):**\n   - When you mark a method `async` and `await _dbContext.Orders.ToListAsync()`, the C# compiler transforms your method into an `IAsyncStateMachine`.\n   - The request starts on Thread #4 and sends the network packet to SQL Server.\n   - As soon as `await` is reached, **Thread #4 is NOT paused or blocked—it is immediately returned to the ThreadPool** so it can process another user's HTTP request!\n   - While SQL Server is working, **zero C# threads** are waiting on that query (the OS network driver handles the I/O completion port).\n   - Once SQL Server responds, a ThreadPool thread picks up the state machine and executes the remaining code after the `await` line.\n3. **Golden Rule:** Never call `.Result` or `.Wait()` on a `Task` (known as *Sync-over-Async*), because it blocks the thread and can cause **Deadlocks** or **ThreadPool Starvation**.\"\n\n### Execution Flow Diagram\n```\nSYNCHRONOUS (Blocking)                     ASYNCHRONOUS (async / await)\nThread #4                                  Thread #4\n  │                                          │\n  ├──► Call SQL DB                           ├──► Call SQL DBAsync()\n  │    (Thread #4 is BLOCKED & IDLE          │    ( hits 'await' -> Thread #4 is FREED\n  │     for 100ms waiting for DB!)           │      back to ThreadPool to serve other users! )\n  │                                          :\n  ├──◄ DB Responds                           ├──◄ DB Responds (OS I/O Completion Interrupt)\n  ▼    Continue code                         ▼    Thread from ThreadPool resumes code after 'await'\n```\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Net.Http;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\nusing Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;\n\npublic class OrderProcessingService\n{\n    private readonly AppDbContext _dbContext;\n    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;\n\n    public OrderProcessingService(AppDbContext dbContext, HttpClient httpClient)\n    {\n        _dbContext = dbContext;\n        _httpClient = httpClient;\n    }\n\n    // Always return Task or Task<T> (Never 'async void' except in UI event handlers!)\n    public async Task<List<Order>> GetRecentOrdersAsync()\n    {\n        // Thread is released back to the ThreadPool while waiting for SQL Server & External API!\n        var exchangeRateJson = await _httpClient.GetStringAsync(\"https://api.example.com/rates\");\n        var orders = await _dbContext.Orders.AsNoTracking().ToListAsync();\n\n        return orders;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q6: What is the difference between a `Task` and a `Thread` in C#?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nA **`Thread`** is a heavy, low-level OS resource (~1 MB stack memory) that executes code, whereas a **`Task`** is a lightweight promise/abstraction representing an asynchronous unit of work that is scheduled onto pooled threads (or runs without any thread during I/O!).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"- **`System.Threading.Thread`:**\n   - Represents an actual **OS-level physical/kernel thread**.\n   - Creating a `new Thread()` is expensive: it allocates around **1 MB of dedicated stack memory** and incurs OS context-switching overhead.\n   - Doesn't easily return a result or chain continuations.\n- **`System.Threading.Tasks.Task` (Task Parallel Library - TPL):**\n   - A higher-level abstraction representing a **unit of work / future result (`Task<T>`)** that will complete in the future.\n   - Instead of spinning up a dedicated OS thread, CPU-bound Tasks reuse existing pre-warmed threads from the **CLR ThreadPool**, and I/O-bound Tasks (`HttpClient`, `EF Core`) don't even occupy a thread while waiting for the network!\n   - Supports `async`/`await`, automatic exception propagation, cancellation via `CancellationToken`, and easy composition via `Task.WhenAll()`.\"\n\n---\n\n## Q7: What is the difference between `Task.WhenAll()` and `Task.WaitAll()`?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`Task.WhenAll()` is **asynchronous and non-blocking** (returns a `Task` that you `await`, freeing the thread while running tasks in parallel), whereas `Task.WaitAll()` is **synchronous and blocks the calling thread** until all tasks finish.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"When we need to call 3 independent microservices or external APIs in parallel:\n1. **`await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2, task3)` (Recommended):**\n   - Runs all tasks concurrently and returns a single `Task` that completes when all input tasks complete.\n   - Because we `await` it, the current thread is **not blocked**—it is released back to the ThreadPool while the 3 operations run concurrently.\n   - If an exception occurs, `await` unwraps and throws the primary exception cleanly.\n2. **`Task.WaitAll(task1, task2, task3)` (Avoid in Web APIs):**\n   - Freezes and **blocks the current thread** until all tasks complete.\n   - Wraps any exceptions inside a clumsy `AggregateException`, and can cause ThreadPool starvation or deadlocks in UI/legacy contexts.\n\n*(5-Year EF Core Warning)*: Never run two queries on the **same `DbContext` instance** inside `Task.WhenAll()`, because `DbContext` is **not thread-safe** and only supports one active operation at a time!\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System.Net.Http;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\npublic class DashboardAggregatorService\n{\n    private readonly HttpClient _http;\n    public DashboardAggregatorService(HttpClient http) => _http = http;\n\n    public async Task<(string Profile, string Orders, string Notifications)> LoadDashboardParallelAsync(int userId)\n    {\n        // Start all 3 HTTP requests concurrently (Do NOT await each line one by one!)\n        Task<string> profileTask = _http.GetStringAsync($\"/api/users/{userId}\");\n        Task<string> ordersTask  = _http.GetStringAsync($\"/api/orders/{userId}\");\n        Task<string> notifTask   = _http.GetStringAsync($\"/api/notifications/{userId}\");\n\n        // ✅ Non-blocking parallel wait: Total time = slowest single API (e.g. 200ms instead of 600ms!)\n        await Task.WhenAll(profileTask, ordersTask, notifTask);\n\n        return (await profileTask, await ordersTask, await notifTask);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q8: In Exception Handling (`try-catch-finally`), does the `finally` block ALWAYS execute even if there is a `return` or an unhandled exception? Can `finally` ever be skipped?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nYes, the `finally` block executes even if there is a `return` statement inside `try`/`catch` or an unhandled exception occurs. It is skipped **only if the process itself crashes or terminates abruptly** (e.g., `Environment.FailFast()`, `Environment.Exit()`, `StackOverflowException`, or power loss).\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"The `finally` block is guaranteed by the CLR to execute whether the `try` block finishes normally, hits an early `return` statement, or throws an exception:\n- **What if `try` has `return 10;`?** The CLR evaluates the return value (`10`), pauses returning, **executes the entire `finally` block first**, and then returns control to the caller. (Note: C# forbids putting a `return` statement inside a `finally` block itself).\n- **When is `finally` NOT executed?** Only when the OS process is killed immediately before the stack can unwind:\n  1. Calling `Environment.FailFast(\"Fatal error\")` or `Environment.Exit(0)`.\n  2. An unrecoverable CLR fatal error like `StackOverflowException` or catastrophic `OutOfMemoryException`.\n  3. Killing the process from Task Manager / Docker container `SIGKILL` / sudden power failure.\"\n\n---\n\n## Q9: What is the difference between `throw;` and `throw ex;` inside a `catch` block?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`throw;` rethrows the original exception while **preserving the full original Stack Trace** (showing the exact line where the bug happened), whereas `throw ex;` **resets and destroys the original Stack Trace**, making it look like the error originated at the `catch` line!\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"This is a critical production debugging question:\n- Suppose a `NullReferenceException` occurs inside `Repository.SaveOrder()` at **Line 45**, and bubbles up to `OrderService.CreateOrder()` where it is caught at **Line 120**.\n- **`throw ex;` (❌ NEVER DO THIS):** Compiles into the `throw` IL instruction, which treats `ex` as a brand-new exception thrown from Line 120. It **wipes out the call stack history**, hiding `Repository.SaveOrder() Line 45` from your Serilog/Application Insights logs!\n- **`throw;` (✅ ALWAYS DO THIS):** Compiles into the `rethrow` IL instruction, which rethrows the exact same exception object with its **complete original Stack Trace intact** so you can see `Repository.SaveOrder() Line 45`.\n- **What if we want to wrap it in a Custom Exception?** Pass `ex` as the **`innerException` parameter**: `throw new OrderProcessingException(\"Failed to save order\", ex);`. That preserves the original stack trace inside `InnerException`.\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;\n\npublic class ExceptionHandlingDemo\n{\n    private readonly ILogger _logger;\n    public ExceptionHandlingDemo(ILogger logger) => _logger = logger;\n\n    public void ProcessPayment()\n    {\n        try\n        {\n            ChargeCard();\n        }\n        catch (Exception ex)\n        {\n            _logger.LogError(ex, \"Payment charge failed.\");\n\n            // ❌ BAD: Resets stack trace to this line! You lose line number inside ChargeCard()!\n            // throw ex;\n\n            // ✅ GOOD Option 1: Rethrow preserving full original stack trace!\n            throw;\n\n            // ✅ GOOD Option 2: Wrap in a domain exception while keeping 'ex' as InnerException\n            // throw new PaymentFailedException(\"Unable to charge customer card.\", ex);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void ChargeCard() => throw new InvalidOperationException(\"Gateway timeout at Line 35\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q10: How do you create a Custom Exception in C#, and how should exceptions be handled in a modern .NET Web API?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nCreate a class that **inherits from `System.Exception`** and provides the three standard constructors (`()`, `(string message)`, `(string message, Exception innerException)`). In Web APIs, catch custom exceptions centrally in a **Global Exception Handling Middleware** instead of writing `try-catch` in every Controller.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"In our projects, we create custom domain exceptions—like `NotFoundException`, `BusinessRuleValidationException`, or `InsufficientBalanceException`—by inheriting from `System.Exception`.\nInstead of polluting every Controller action with repetitive `try-catch` blocks, we throw these custom exceptions from our Service layer and catch them in a single **Global Exception Handling Middleware** (or `IExceptionHandler` in .NET 8). The middleware inspects the exception type and maps it to a standard `ProblemDetails` HTTP status code (e.g., `NotFoundException` $\\rightarrow$ `404 Not Found`, `BusinessRuleValidationException` $\\rightarrow$ `400 Bad Request`, unhandled exceptions $\\rightarrow$ `500 Internal Server Error`).\"\n\n### Code Example\n```csharp\nusing System;\n\n// Custom Domain Exception following Microsoft guidelines\npublic class InsufficientStockException : Exception\n{\n    public string Sku { get; }\n\n    public InsufficientStockException() { }\n\n    public InsufficientStockException(string message)\n        : base(message) { }\n\n    public InsufficientStockException(string message, Exception innerException)\n        : base(message, innerException) { }\n\n    public InsufficientStockException(string sku, int requested, int available)\n        : base($\"Product '{sku}' has only {available} units in stock (Requested: {requested}).\")\n    {\n        Sku = sku;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q11: How does the .NET Garbage Collector (GC) work? Explain Generations (Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2) and LOH.\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\nThe .NET Garbage Collector manages Heap memory by dividing objects into **3 Generations (`Gen 0`, `Gen 1`, `Gen 2`)** based on their survival age—cleaning short-lived objects (`Gen 0`) very frequently and long-lived objects (`Gen 2`) rarely.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"The .NET Garbage Collector uses a **Generational Mark-and-Compact algorithm** based on the observation that most objects in an application (like HTTP request DTOs, local strings, and LINQ iterators) die very young:\n1. **Generation 0 (Short-Lived Objects):**\n   - Every newly allocated small object (`< 85,000 bytes`) starts in **Gen 0**.\n   - Gen 0 is very small (fits in CPU cache) and is collected **most frequently**. When a HTTP request finishes, 99% of its temporary objects are no longer referenced and are wiped out in a sub-millisecond Gen 0 collection.\n   - Any object that **survives** a Gen 0 collection is promoted to **Gen 1**.\n2. **Generation 1 (Buffer Generation):**\n   - Acts as a staging buffer between short-lived Gen 0 objects and long-lived Gen 2 objects.\n   - Objects that survive a Gen 1 collection are promoted to **Gen 2**.\n3. **Generation 2 (Long-Lived Objects):**\n   - Holds objects that live for a long time—such as `static` data, Singleton services, and in-memory caches.\n   - Gen 2 collections (called **Full GC**) are expensive because they scan the entire Managed Heap, so the CLR runs them rarely.\n4. **LOH (Large Object Heap):**\n   - Any single object **85,000 bytes (85 KB) or larger** (usually large `byte[]` arrays or huge strings) skips Gen 0 and Gen 1 and is allocated directly on the **Large Object Heap (LOH)** (collected only during Gen 2 GCs) because copying 85 KB+ objects during memory compaction is too expensive.\"\n\n### GC Generations Diagram\n```\n[ new Object() (< 85 KB) ]\n         │\n         ▼\n┌──────────────────────┐   Survives GC   ┌──────────────────────┐   Survives GC   ┌──────────────────────┐\n│    GENERATION 0      │ ──────────────► │    GENERATION 1      │ ──────────────► │    GENERATION 2      │\n│ Short-lived objects  │                 │ Buffer / Transitional│                 │ Long-lived Singletons│\n│ (HTTP DTOs, locals)  │                 │       objects        │                 │ Static data, Caches  │\n│ Collected Frequently │                 │                      │                 │ Collected Rarely     │\n└──────────────────────┘                 └──────────────────────┘                 └──────────────────────┘\n                                                                                             ▲\n[ new byte[100_000] (>= 85 KB) ] ──► Allocated directly in LOH (Large Object Heap) ──────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q12: What is the difference between `Finalize()` (Destructor) and `Dispose()` (`IDisposable`)? How does the `using` statement work?\n\n### Simple Meaning (1-2 lines)\n`Dispose()` (`IDisposable`) is called **deterministically and immediately by the developer** (or via a `using` block) to free unmanaged resources right away, whereas `Finalize()` (`~ClassName()`) is called **non-deterministically by the Garbage Collector** at an unknown time in the future as a backup safety net.\n\n### Detailed Interview Answer (What to Speak)\n\"The Garbage Collector automatically frees **Managed Heap memory**, but it has **no idea** how to release **Unmanaged Resources** like open SQL database connections, OS file handles, network sockets, or memory streams:\n1. **`IDisposable.Dispose()` (Deterministic Cleanup):**\n   - We implement `IDisposable` and call `.Dispose()` (or wrap the object in a **`using` statement**) so the unmanaged resource (like a database connection or locked file) is closed **the exact microsecond** our method finishes.\n   - Under the hood, the C# compiler translates `using (var conn = new SqlConnection(...))` into a **`try { ... } finally { conn?.Dispose(); }`** block, guaranteeing cleanup even if an exception is thrown!\n   - Inside `Dispose()`, we also call `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` to tell the Garbage Collector: *'I already cleaned up the unmanaged resources manually, so don't waste time putting this object in the Finalization Queue!'*\n2. **`Finalize()` / C# Destructor `~ClassName()` (Non-Deterministic Cleanup):**\n   - Written using tilde syntax `~MyResource()`, which compiles to `Finalize()`.\n   - You **cannot call it manually**; the GC calls it on a background Finalizer thread before reclaiming the object's memory—which might happen seconds or minutes later.\n   - Objects with a Finalizer automatically survive their first GC collection and get promoted to the next generation, making them slower to collect. It is only used as a safety fallback if a developer forgets to call `Dispose()`.\"\n\n### Comparison Table\n| Feature | `Dispose()` (`IDisposable`) | `Finalize()` (`~ClassName()`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Who calls it?** | Called **explicitly by code** or via **`using` block** | Called **automatically by the Garbage Collector** |\n| **When does it run?** | **Immediately / Deterministically** when scope exits | **Non-deterministically** (Whenever GC runs in the future) |\n| **Performance Impact** | Fast! Frees resources immediately; calls `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` | Slow! Promotes object to next GC Generation via Finalization Queue |\n| **How to use in C#** | `using var stream = new FileStream(...);` | Only needed when wrapping raw OS pointers (`IntPtr`) directly |\n\n### Code Example (The `using` Statement & Dispose Pattern)\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing Microsoft.Data.SqlClient;\n\npublic class DatabaseReaderDemo\n{\n    public void ExecuteQuery(string connectionString)\n    {\n        // Modern C# 8+ 'using declaration':\n        // Automatically compiles to try/finally and calls conn.Dispose() at the end of the method!\n        using var conn = new SqlConnection(connectionString);\n        conn.Open();\n\n        using var cmd = new SqlCommand(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders\", conn);\n        int count = (int)cmd.ExecuteScalar();\n        Console.WriteLine($\"Total Orders: {count}\");\n    } // <-- cmd.Dispose() and conn.Dispose() are guaranteed to execute right here, even on Exception!\n}\n```\n";

window.INTERVIEW_LESSONS["csharp-05"] = "# C# Lesson 5: Modern C# (`record`, Extension Methods, Tuples) & Multithreading (`lock`, Deadlocks, `CancellationToken`)\n\n---\n\n## Q1: What is an Extension Method in C#? Where have you used it?\n* **Simple Meaning:** An Extension Method lets you **add a new method to an existing class** (like `string`, `int`, or `IServiceCollection`) **without modifying the original class's source code or inheriting from it** (especially helpful when the class is `sealed` like `string`!).\n* **3 Rules to Write an Extension Method:**\n  1. The class must be a **`public static class`**.\n  2. The method must be a **`public static` method**.\n  3. The **first parameter** must have the **`this`** keyword before the type you are extending (`this string input`).\n* **Interview Answer (What to Speak):**\n  > \"Extension methods allow us to add new methods to existing types without modifying, recompiling, or inheriting from the original type. In fact, all **LINQ** methods like `.Where()`, `.Select()`, and `.ToList()` are built-in Extension Methods on `IEnumerable<T>`! In my ASP.NET Core MVC project, we also write extension methods on `IServiceCollection` (like `builder.Services.AddApplicationServices()`) to keep `Program.cs` clean.\"\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\npublic static class StringExtensions\n{\n    // Notice the 'this' keyword before string!\n    public static bool IsValidIndianMobile(this string mobile)\n    {\n        return !string.IsNullOrEmpty(mobile) && mobile.Length == 10 && mobile.All(char.IsDigit);\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage anywhere in project:\nstring phone = \"9876543210\";\nbool isValid = phone.IsValidIndianMobile(); // Looks like a built-in method on string!\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q2: What is a `record` in C# (C# 9+)? How is `record` different from `class`?\n* **Simple Meaning:** A `record` is a special reference type designed for **immutable Data Transfer Objects (DTOs)** where equality is checked by **comparing property values** instead of memory addresses!\n* **Comparison Table (`class` vs `record`):**\n\n| Feature | `class` | `record` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Equality (`==` and `.Equals()`)** | Compares **Memory Reference** (`obj1 == obj2` is `false` even if properties match) | Compares **Values** (`rec1 == rec2` is `true` if all property values match!) |\n| **Mutability** | Mutable by default (`{ get; set; }`) | Immutable by default (`{ get; init; }` via positional syntax) |\n| **Cloning (`with` keyword)** | Must manually copy properties | Supports non-destructive copying using `var rec2 = rec1 with { Salary = 90000 };` |\n| **`.ToString()` output** | Prints only class name (`MyApp.Employee`) | Automatically prints all property names and values (`Employee { Id = 1, Name = Raj }`) |\n| **Best Use Case** | Services, Controllers, Repositories, EF Entities | **API Request/Response DTOs**, Events, Read-only Config |\n\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\n// One-line immutable DTO using record!\npublic record EmployeeDto(int Id, string Name, decimal Salary);\n\nvar e1 = new EmployeeDto(101, \"Rahul\", 50000);\nvar e2 = new EmployeeDto(101, \"Rahul\", 50000);\n\nConsole.WriteLine(e1 == e2); // TRUE! (In a normal class, this would be FALSE!)\n\n// Create a modified copy using 'with' expression:\nvar promoted = e1 with { Salary = 75000 };\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q3: What is the difference between `Tuple`, `ValueTuple`, and `Anonymous Type`?\n* **Anonymous Type (`new { Id = 1, Name = \"Raj\" }`):**\n  * Created on the fly (often in LINQ `.Select(x => new { x.Id, x.Name })`).\n  * **Limitation:** Cannot be returned from a method signature (without `object`/`dynamic`) because it has no type name.\n* **`ValueTuple` (`(int Id, string Name)` — C# 7+):**\n  * A lightweight **value type (`struct`)** that lets a method return **multiple values easily** without creating a separate class or using `out` parameters!\n* **Code Example (`ValueTuple` returning multiple values):**\n```csharp\npublic (bool IsSuccess, string ErrorMessage, int OrderId) PlaceOrder(OrderDto dto)\n{\n    if (dto.Amount <= 0)\n        return (false, \"Invalid amount\", 0);\n\n    return (true, string.Empty, 9001);\n}\n\n// Calling the method:\nvar result = PlaceOrder(myOrder);\nif (result.IsSuccess)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"Order created: {result.OrderId}\");\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q4: What are Indexers in C#?\n* **Simple Meaning:** Indexers allow an object of your custom class to be indexed using square brackets `obj[0]` or `obj[\"key\"]` just like an Array or `Dictionary`!\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\npublic class WeekSchedule\n{\n    private readonly string[] _tasks = new string[7];\n\n    // Indexer defined using 'this[int dayIndex]'\n    public string this[int dayIndex]\n    {\n        get => _tasks[dayIndex];\n        set => _tasks[dayIndex] = value;\n    }\n}\n\nvar schedule = new WeekSchedule();\nschedule[0] = \"Sprint Planning\";\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q5: What are Attributes and Reflection in C#?\n* **Attributes (`[HttpGet]`, `[Required]`, `[Authorize]`, `[Obsolete]`):** Declarative metadata tags placed above classes, methods, or properties.\n* **Reflection (`System.Reflection`):** The ability of a C# program to **inspect its own metadata, types, properties, and attributes at runtime** (and even invoke methods dynamically).\n* **Where used in real projects:** ASP.NET Core uses Reflection + Attributes at startup to find all `[ApiController]` classes, check `[Required]` validations, and map columns in Entity Framework / AutoMapper!\n\n---\n\n## Q6: What is Multithreading vs Asynchronous Programming (`Thread` vs `Task`)?\n* **Simple Meaning:**\n  * **Multithreading (`Thread` / `Parallel.ForEach`):** Uses **multiple CPU cores** simultaneously to run heavy **CPU-bound calculations** (e.g., resizing 1,000 images or heavy math).\n  * **Asynchronous Programming (`async` / `await` with `Task`):** Does **NOT** create new threads for waiting! When waiting for **I/O-bound work** (like an Oracle DB query or external HTTP API call), it **releases the thread back to the .NET ThreadPool** so the server can handle other users' requests.\n\n---\n\n## Q7: What is Thread Safety and Race Condition? How does `lock` solve it?\n* **Simple Meaning:**\n  * **Race Condition:** When 2 or more threads read and modify the same shared variable (like `static int _counter` or a non-thread-safe `List<T>`) at the exact same millisecond, corrupting the data.\n  * **`lock` Keyword:** Ensures that **only ONE thread at a time** can enter the critical code block while other threads wait in line.\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\npublic class InvoiceNumberGenerator\n{\n    private static int _lastNumber = 1000;\n    private static readonly object _lockObj = new object();\n\n    public int GetNextInvoiceNumber()\n    {\n        // Only ONE thread can enter this block at a time!\n        lock (_lockObj)\n        {\n            _lastNumber++;\n            return _lastNumber;\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n> **Important Trap:** You **cannot** use `await` inside a `lock (...)` block! If you need to lock around an `async/await` method, use **`SemaphoreSlim(1, 1)`** (`await _semaphore.WaitAsync();`).\n\n---\n\n## Q8: What is a Deadlock in C# and how do you prevent it?\n* **Simple Meaning:**\n  * **Classic Thread Deadlock:** Thread 1 holds `Lock A` and waits for `Lock B`, while Thread 2 holds `Lock B` and waits forever for `Lock A`. Neither can ever finish!\n  * **`async/await` Sync-over-Async Deadlock (Classic Legacy ASP.NET / WinForms Bug):** Calling `.Result` or `.Wait()` on an `async` method (`var data = GetDataAsync().Result;`). The main thread blocks waiting for the task, while the `await` inside `GetDataAsync()` waits for the blocked main thread to become free!\n* **How to Prevent It:**\n  1. **Never** call `.Result` or `.Wait()` on async tasks—use `await` all the way (`async` all the way up).\n  2. In library code, use `.ConfigureAwait(false)`.\n  3. Always acquire locks in a fixed, consistent order or use timeouts.\n\n---\n\n## Q9: What is a `CancellationToken` in ASP.NET Core and why should you pass it to async methods?\n* **Simple Meaning:** Suppose a user clicks \"Generate Big Report\" (which runs a 15-second Oracle query), and after 2 seconds the user **closes their browser tab** or navigates away. Without `CancellationToken`, your server wastes CPU and Oracle database resources finishing a 15-second query that nobody is waiting for!\n* **Interview Answer (What to Speak):**\n  > \"A `CancellationToken` allows cooperative cancellation of asynchronous operations. In ASP.NET Core Controllers, if we add `CancellationToken cancellationToken` as an action parameter and pass it down to EF Core (`ToListAsync(cancellationToken)`) or `HttpClient`, ASP.NET Core automatically triggers the token if the user aborts the HTTP request or closes the browser, immediately cancelling the database query and freeing server resources.\"\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\n[HttpGet(\"orders\")]\npublic async Task<IActionResult> GetOrders(CancellationToken cancellationToken)\n{\n    // If user closes browser, EF Core cancels the SQL query automatically!\n    var orders = await _dbContext.Orders\n        .AsNoTracking()\n        .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);\n\n    return Ok(orders);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Q10: What is the difference between `Task` and `ValueTask`?\n* **Simple Meaning:**\n  * `Task<T>` is a **Reference Type (`class`)**, so every time an async method returns a `Task<T>`, it allocates an object on the Heap.\n  * `ValueTask<T>` is a **Value Type (`struct`)**. If a method returns data **synchronously from cache 95% of the time** and only hits the database 5% of the time, returning `ValueTask<T>` avoids unnecessary Heap memory allocations and reduces Garbage Collection pressure!\n\n---\n\n## Q11: What is `Span<T>` and `ReadOnlySpan<T>` in Modern .NET?\n* **Simple Meaning:** Allows slicing strings or arrays (`ReadOnlySpan<char> slice = fullString.AsSpan(0, 5);`) **with ZERO Heap memory allocation** instead of `.Substring(0, 5)` (which creates a brand-new string on the Heap every time).\n\n---\n\n## Q12: What are Primary Constructors (C# 12 / .NET 8)?\n* **Simple Meaning:** In C# 12 (.NET 8), you can put constructor parameters directly on the class declaration line (`public class OrderController(IOrderService orderService) : ControllerBase`), eliminating the boilerplate of declaring private fields and assigning them inside a constructor body!\n* **Code Example:**\n```csharp\n// C# 12 Primary Constructor: Clean & concise DI!\npublic class OrderController(IOrderService orderService, ILogger<OrderController> logger) : ControllerBase\n{\n    [HttpGet(\"{id}\")]\n    public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)\n    {\n        logger.LogInformation(\"Fetching order {Id}\", id);\n        return Ok(await orderService.GetByIdAsync(id));\n    }\n}\n```\n";
